text_mt:
When one establishes the fire,
he should offer afterwards an eleven-pot-sherd cake to Indra and Agni,
and a boiled rice mess to Aditi.
Indra and Agni are the unexhausted ones among the gods;
whichever deities are unexhausted,
from both of them he secures sensual power and heroic power for him.
It belongs to Aditi;
Aditi is this [earth];
he firmly establishes himself on this [earth].
This is the semen of the milch cow,

namely the clarified butter;
the rice grains belong to the draft ox;
he secures a pair.
It is in ghee,
so that the sacrifice does not become dry.
Four descendants of ṛṣis
eat it;
he offers [it] in the light of the quarters of space.
These oblations are domestic animals;
this is Rudra,
namely Agni.

just as one would fill three vessels,
such would that be;
he would not leave any room for procreation.
Having offered one,
he should combine the subsequent two;
he leaves a third realm for him for procreation;
he procreates after it with offspring and domestic animals.
Moreover, this is the forward stride of the sacrifice.
He rolls forward a chariot wheel;
by means of the human chariot he mounts down to the divine chariot.

He pleases the seasons, who have Agni as their mouth.
He gives a headrest,
for the obtaining of forms;
a horse to the brahman priest,
he secures sensual power;
a milch cow to the hotṛ,
he secures blessings;
a draft ox to the adhvaryu;
the draft ox is a draft-beast;
the adhvaryu is a draft-beast.

By means of a draft-beast he secures the draft-beast of the sacrifice.
He gives a pair of cattle,
for the obtaining of a pair.
He gives a garment;
the garment belongs to all the gods;
he pleases all the gods.
He gives up to twelve [gifts];
twelve months are the year;
he firmly establishes himself in the year.
At will more may be given,
for the obtaining of the boundless.

'Heat is the head: so this Agni;
may he become well-beloved by the domestic animals;
extend protection to offspring and descendants.
Wind is exhalation: so this Agni;
may he become well-beloved by the domestic animals;
cook seasoned food for offspring and descendants.
Go forth along the eastern direction, knowing,
O Agni, be Agni in front of Agni here;
shining, shine out in all regions;
bestow vigor upon our biped and quadruped.'

These worlds indeed are these fires.
If they were established without distinction,
they would cause suffering to the sacrificer.
With 'Heat is the head,' he establishes the gārhapatya;
with 'Wind is exhalation,' the anvāhāryapacana;
with 'The sun is the eye,' the āhavanīya.
Thereby he distinguishes them;
thus they do not cause suffering to the sacrificer.
He sings the Rathantara over the gārhapatya as it is being established;
this world belongs to the Rathantara.

He establishes it firmly established in this world.
He sings the Vāmadevya as it is being carried out;
the intermediate space is the Vāmadevya;
he establishes it firmly established in the intermediate space.
Moreover, the Vāmadevya is appeasement;
appeased, rich in cattle, he carries it out.
He sings the Bṛhat over the āhavanīya as it is being established;
yonder world belongs to the Bṛhat;
he establishes it firmly established in yonder world.
Prajāpati created Agni.

a leaf fell from the third heaven;
that is this parṇa tree, born indeed from the soma-leaf;
from it I take [a stick] for obtaining the drinking of soma.
When you listened to the gods
speaking the discussion of sacred lore, you are indeed renowned as Suśravas;
from that let spiritual preeminence enter me;
bringing it together, may I obtain that perceptibly.
By which, when Agni was created,
Prajāpati appeased his weapon,
that very [branch of] śamī for non-burning,

He who, knowing thus, sacrifices with the Vājapeya
goes to independent sovereignty;
he attains the foremost place among his equals;
they give way to him for preeminence.
Indeed, this is the sacrifice of both the Brahmin and the Rājanya.
Indeed, they call it the Vājapeya.
Indeed, this is attainable through vigor;
for by this the gods sought to obtain vigor.
The Vājapeya is indeed Soma.
He who knows Soma as the Vājapeya,

having drunk it, he becomes vigorous indeed;
a vigorous one is born to him.
The Vājapeya is indeed food.
He who knows thus
eats food;
an eater of food is born to him.
The Vājapeya is indeed the brahman.
He who knows thus
eats food through the brahman;
a knower of brahman is born to him.

Therefore, one should not accept [gifts] from a singer or an intoxicated person.
If one should accept,
one would accept impurity.
All speeches indeed are obtained for him
who is a sacrificer with the Vājapeya:
that which is on earth, in Agni, in the Rathantara;
that which is in the intermediate space, in Vāyu, in the Vāmadevya;
that which is in heaven, in the Sun, in the Bṛhat;
that which is in the waters, in the plants, in the trees.
Therefore, the sacrificer with the Vājapeya is fit for the priesthood;
for all speeches are obtained for him.

"May I be united with offspring, may offspring be united with me," he says;
this very blessing he seeks.
They strike [him] with small packages of grain;
this [earth] is food;
they enrich him with food.
They strike [him] with salty earth;
for these are visibly food,
namely, the salty earths;
they visibly enrich him with food.
They strike [him] from the front towards the west;

for food is eaten from the front towards the back.
They strike [him] from the head;
for food is eaten from the head.
They strike [him] from the quarters;
from the quarters they secure food for him.
He is liable to perish downwards,
who mounts the sacrificial post.
He steps down onto gold;
gold is immortality;
the heavenly realm is immortality;
he is established in the immortal, heavenly realm.
It weighs a hundred [measures];
man has a hundred-year lifespan, a hundred faculties;
he is established in lifespan, in faculty.
This indeed is the form of nourishment,
namely, a goat;
she gives birth three times a year, beyond other domestic animals.
He steps down onto a he-goat skin;
he is established in nourishment, in procreation.

He offers seven food-oblations;
there are seven foods;
as many foods as there are,
those very ones he secures.
Seven are cultivated plants,
seven are wild;
for the securing of both.
He offers oblation of every food,
for the securing of every food.
If the performer of the Vājapeya should eat of what has not been secured,

he would be deprived of what has been secured.
Having cut off a portion from each, he offers,
for the securing of what has not been secured.
He offers with a ladle of udumbara wood;
the udumbara is vigor, food;
for the securing of vigor, of food.
"At the instigation of divine Savitṛ," he says;
impelled by Savitṛ, he sprinkles him with brahman, with deities.
He sprinkles with every food,
for the securing of every food.

He sprinkles [him] from the front facing west;
for food is eaten from the front towards the back.
He sprinkles from the head;
for food is eaten from the head.
He lets it stream down up to the mouth;
he places food into him right from the mouth.
"With the universal sovereignty of Agni I sprinkle you," he says;
this indeed is Agni's unction;
with that very [power] he sprinkles him.
"With the universal sovereignty of Indra I sprinkle you," he says;

faculty he thereby places in him.
"With the universal sovereignty of Bṛhaspati I sprinkle you," he says;
Bṛhaspati is the brahman among the gods;
with brahman he sprinkles him.
They bring the soma cups and the offering portions to the priests;
that other realm he makes full of food through them.
And [they bring] the liquor cups and the non-offering portions to the racers;
this realm he makes full of food through them.
He is sprinkled with both;
the racers make a scuffle,

for the securing of faculty.
They chant the praise with unexpressed [verses] at the morning pressing;
Prajāpati is unexpressed;
for the attainment of Prajāpati.
With [verses] containing [the word] "speed" at midday;
speed is food;
food he secures.
With [verses] containing [the word] "Śipiviṣṭa" at the third pressing;
sacrifice is Viṣṇu;
domestic animals are the śipi-flesh;
the sacrifice is established in domestic animals.
The Bṛhat [chant] is the final one;
he brings him to the very end of prosperity.

"You that sit in the waters, you that sit in the ghee," he says;
he is consecrated over waters and over ghee by this.
"You that sit in the sky," he says;
when one becomes wealthier,
they say of him: "He has reached the sky";
he reaches the sky by this.
"You that sit on earth, you that sit in the intermediate space," he says;
he is consecrated over these realms by this.
Therefore, the performer of the Vājapeya descends before no one;
for he is consecrated, as it were, over the deities.
"You that sit in heaven," he says;
when one becomes wealthier,
they say of him: "He has reached heaven";
he reaches heaven by this.
"Which cups belong to the five peoples," he says;
he is consecrated over the five peoples by this.
"The sap of the waters, highest vigor," he says;
he is consecrated over the sap of the waters by this.
"Brought together with the sun's ray," he says, for the sake of clear brightness.

They gave it back to them.
Therefore, [the rite] is performed for the ancestors on the day before.
When he performs [it] for the ancestors on the day before,
having redeemed the sacrifice from the ancestors, the sacrificer stretches it out.
"To Soma accompanied by the ancestors, svadhā, reverence!" he says;
he secures the drink of soma from the father.
For a father, when dying, does not say: "Here is the drink of soma."
The drink of soma is faculty;
faculty, the drink of soma, he secures;
with that faculty he attains a second wife.

Bestow upon us wealth winning prize-food, rich in good heroes,
praiseworthy, glorious, great;
in whom horses, bulls, oxen,
barren cows, and rams are let loose, sacrificed,
to the drinker of sweet drink, soma-backed, the disposer,
to Agni generate from the heart a pleasing thought.
For separately measured has been your seat ordained by the gods;
may you two not commingle in the highest heaven.
Thou art surā, foaming; this is soma;
harm me not as thou enterest thy own womb.

for victory, for glory,
for vigor, for offspring," he says.
Domestic animals indeed are wealth;
he makes the domestic animals rest for him.
"May the two springs of Sarasvatī kindle you," he says.
The Ṛg and Sāman indeed are the two springs of Sarasvatī;
with the Ṛg and Sāman indeed he kindles him.
"You are the sovereign, you are the far-shining," he says.
The Rathantara indeed is the sovereign;
the Bṛhat is the far-shining.

They perform on [verses] addressed to the Maruts;
by that indeed they do not depart from the midday pressing.
They draw [soma] into the Hotṛ's cup;
the Hotṛ recites along.
In the middle indeed he connects the sacrifice.
For whom soma remains over at the midday pressing,
it remains over as desiring the third pressing of the Ādityas.
The sāman is the Gaurīvita;
the Gaurīvita indeed is redundant,
redundant is what remains over from a pressing.

[It is] for the appeasement of the redundant.
"Truly you are great, O Sūrya"—they perform [with this].
With whatever desire of the Ādityas' pressing it remains over,
with that very desire they make him prosper.
The sāman is the Gaurīvita;
by that indeed they do not depart from the midday pressing.
The stoma is the seventeenfold;
by that indeed they do not depart from the third pressing.
They draw [soma] into the Hotṛ's cup;
the Hotṛ recites along.

The opening verses are addressed to the Maruts;
the Maruts indeed are the unconquered abode of the gods;
he strives in the unconquered abode of the gods.
Both the Bṛhat and Rathantara [sāmans] are used.
This [earth] is the Rathantara;
that [sky] is the Bṛhat.
By these two indeed he separates him [the rival]:
from speech and mind,
from exhalation and inhalation,
from sky and earth.

For thus the gods did;
thus the gods did;
and thus indeed men do.
The gods said:
'If we perform this sacrifice aloud,
then the asuras, evil, will find us.
Let us perform the upasads silently;
thus the asuras, evil, will not find us.'
They performed the upasad silently,
having recited only three kindling verses (sāmidhenīs).

having offered three successive oblations,
offers the upasad with the dipping spoon:
'Fierce speech I have struck down, violent speech I have struck down, svāhā.'
Hunger and thirst are indeed fierce speech;
sin and the slaying of an enemy are violent speech.
Verily, that fourfold evil the sacrificer strikes away.
Having brought [the day] to a close, they seized the victim;
thereby the gods struck away destitution, evil, [and] death from the day.
As if having brought [it] to a close, they proceeded from the night;
thereby the gods struck away destitution, evil, [and] death from the night.

They praised towards him with the seventeenfold [stoma];
they took him with the seventeenfold;
they bore him with the seventeenfold,
as great as is the measure of the seventeenfold.
While he was being borne by the seventeenfold, his fiery energy, his brilliant glow fell away.
They praised towards him with the twenty-onefold [stoma];
they took him with the twenty-onefold;
they bore him with the twenty-onefold,
as great as is the measure of the twenty-onefold.
When they chant praise with the ninefold,

He offers in a natural barren spot or in a cleft;
for this is the abode of Nirṛti.
In her own abode he appeases Nirṛti by offering a portion.
'This is your share, o Nirṛti,' he says;
he assigns [it] to her.
'You are prosperous, rich in oblations,' he says;
he turns towards prosperity.
'Release this one from distress,' he says;
he releases him from distress.
He offers with the two thumbs.

'Sacrifice to me first;
with me as the mouth you shall conquer the asuras.'
'To me second,' Soma said;
'with me as king you shall conquer.'
'To me third,' Savitṛ [said];
'impelled by me you shall conquer.'
'To me fourth,' Sarasvatī [said];
'I shall place physical power in you.'
'To me fifth,' Pūṣan [said];
'with me as firm foundation you shall conquer.'

With Agni as the mouth they conquered the asuras,
with Soma as king,
impelled by Savitṛ;
Sarasvatī placed physical power [in them];
Pūṣan was the firm foundation.
Then indeed the gods were victorious.
In that these oblations are poured out, it is for victory.
He does not heap up the high altar;
for the high altar is domestic animals,
and domestic animals are, as it were, unborn at that time.

If he were to offer on the same day,
he would secure only as much wish as one secures with a single oblation.
He offers day after day;
he secures indeed a greater wish;
he attains more sacrificial rites.
He offers a caru to Bṛhaspati at the home of the Brahmin priest (brahman);
right from the mouth he sharpens regal power with brahman for him;
moreover, he causes regal power to grasp hold of brahman.
A white-backed [ox] is the sacrificial fee, for success.

An eleven-pot-sherd [cake] to Indra at the home of the Rājanya;
he secures Indra-power itself.
A bull is the sacrificial fee, for success.
A caru to Aditi at the home of the chief queen (mahiṣī);
Aditi is indeed this [earth];
he establishes himself on this [earth].
A milch cow is the sacrificial fee, for success.
A caru to Bhaga at the home of the favorite wife (vāvātā);
he places fortune into him.
A six-month-pregnant four-year-old cow is the sacrificial fee, for success.

A caru to Nirṛti at the home of the discarded wife (parivṛktī), [made] of black rice grain split with fingernails;
he appeases Nirṛti, evil itself.
A black, hornless cow is the sacrificial fee, for success.
An eight-pot-sherd [cake] to Agni at the home of the commander of the army (senānī);
he sharpens his army.
Gold is the sacrificial fee, for success.
A ten-pot-sherd [cake] to Varuṇa at the home of the charioteer/herald (sūta);
he secures the consecration of Varuṇa.
A castrated bull is the sacrificial fee, for success.
A seven-pot-sherd [cake] to the Maruts at the home of the village headman (grāmaṇī);

the Maruts are food;
he secures food.
A dappled [cow] is the sacrificial fee, for success.
A twelve-pot-sherd [cake] to Savitṛ at the home of the chamberlain (kṣattṛ), for impulse;
a grey-speckled [animal] is the sacrificial fee, for success.
A two-pot-sherd [cake] to the Aśvins at the home of the charioteer (saṃgrahītṛ);
the two Aśvins are indeed the physicians of the gods;
through them he prepares medicine for him.
Two twin calves are the sacrificial fee, for success.
A caru to Pūṣan at the home of the carver/collector (bhāgadugha);

Pūṣan is food;
he secures food.
A black [animal] is the sacrificial fee, for success.
A caru made of gavidhukā grass to Rudra at the home of the superintendent of dice (akṣāvāpa);
at the end he appeases Rudra.
A speckled ox with raised horns is the sacrificial fee, for success.
These are twelve oblations.
The year has twelve months;
through the year he secures the realm for him.
He indeed comes to have a realm.

He covers the offering to Bṛhaspati with the offering to Mitra;
he places brahman and regal power together in harmony for him;
moreover, he establishes regal power firmly in brahman.
He proceeds first with the offering to Bṛhaspati;
right from the mouth he sharpens brahman for him;
moreover, he causes regal power to grasp hold of brahman.
The altar is self-made;
the sacrificial grass is self-cut;
the kindling wood is self-made;
for the conquest of what is unconquered.
Therefore the forest is conquered for kings.
That same white cow with a white calf is the sacrificial fee, for success.

These are the oblations of the divine quickeners (devasūs).
There are so many consecrations of the gods;
they bestow consecrations upon him;
they consecrate him.
Agni consecrates him [as lord] of householders;
Soma of lord of the trees;
Rudra of domestic animals;
Bṛhaspati of speech;
Indra of the elders;
Mitra of truths;

Varuṇa of the lords of sacred law (dharma).
This becomes everything.
'May Savitṛ quicken you among the impulses,' [saying this] he takes [his] hand for impulse.
'You gods who are the divine quickeners,' he says;
this is strictly according to the yajus.
'For great regal power, for great overlordship, for great realm of the people,' he says;
he expresses this very wish.
'This is your king, O Bharatas; Soma is the king of us Brahmins,' he says;
therefore Brahmins have Soma as king.
'This royal power has been established in its name,' he says;

he firmly places royal power in him.
'Varuṇa has laid his own body upon [him],' he says;
he secures the consecration of Varuṇa.
'We have become devotees of pure Mitra,' he says;
he makes him pure and observant of vows.
'We have thought on the name of the great cosmic order (ṛta),' he says;
he honors him.
'All the troops have become Varuṇa's,' he says;
he makes him one whose troops are all.
'Mitra has overcome enmity with his courses,' he says;

he makes him overcome enmity.
'The sacrificial ones have gladdened [him] by the cosmic order,' he says;
he sweetens him.
'Trita has thoroughly reached our old age,' he says;
he places lifespan into him.
He wipes [his hands] with two [formulas];
two-footed is the sacrificer, for firm standing.
He cuts off portions together for Agni Sviṣṭakṛt from the eleven-pot-sherd [cake] for Agni-Soma and from the oblations of the divine quickeners;
he encloses him on both sides with deities.
He strides the Viṣṇu-strides;
becoming Viṣṇu himself, he conquers these worlds.

'You have arrived for the purpose,' [saying this] he offers an oblation;
by means of the oblation he redeems them and takes [them].
Moreover, he draws [water] from those [waters] that are made into sacrificial matter and sprinkled with clarified butter.
He draws [waters] that are flowing;
these indeed are the realm of the waters;
he draws the realm for him.
Moreover, they bear him to prosperity.
'You are the lord of the waters,' he says;
he has produced a pair.
'You are a virile wave,' he says;

He should offer in her house, reciting: 'O Prajāpati, other than you none [has encompassed all these creations],'—
[in the house of her] of whom he desires that her offspring should be the realm.
Her offspring becomes the realm.
The Adhvaryu anoints with [a cup] made of parṇa-wood;
he places spiritual preeminence and brilliance in him.
A Rājanya [anoints] with [a cup] of udumbara-wood;
he places vigor and food in him.
A Vaiśya [anoints] with [a cup] of aśvattha-wood;
he places the people and nourishment in him.
A clansman [anoints] with [a cup] of nyagrodha-wood;
he prepares friends for him,
and also for firm standing.

Six are the seasons;
with the seasons he yokes it.
He takes the strides of Viṣṇu;
becoming Viṣṇu he conquers these worlds.
The kṣatriya who is set in place
takes hold of him from behind;
he becomes the realm.
He takes hold from behind with a triṣṭubh;
the triṣṭubh is sensory power;
he places sensory power in the sacrificer.

'Homage to mother earth,' he says, for non-injury.
'Thou art so much, thou art lifespan, bestow lifespan on me,' he says;
he places vigor into himself.
'Thou art vigor, bestow vigor on me,' he says;
he places vigor into himself.
'Thou art yoked, thou art luster, bestow luster upon me,' he says;
he places luster into himself.
He brings [these offerings] together to the brahman priest in a single act;
in a single act the sacrificer thus bestows lifespan, vigor, and luster [upon himself].
He offers the chariot-unloosing oblations for firm standing.

There are three horses;
the chariot is the fourth.
Therefore he makes four offerings.
If both were to step down together,
they would go to the same world.
Together with the charioteer he places the chariot on the chariot-stand;
he thereby screens him from the heavenly realm.
'The swan seated in the clear [sky],' with this [verse] he places it;
he brings it down with the brahman;
he places it with the brahman.
He places it with an aticchandas verse;
verily, the aticchandas is all the meters;
he places it with all the meters.
This is the summit of the meters,
namely the aticchandas;
because he places it with an aticchandas,
he makes him the summit among his equals.

'Thou art Mitra, thou art Varuṇa,' he says.
Day belongs to Mitra;
night belongs to Varuṇa.
By both day and night he brings him down.
'Thou art Mitra, thou art Varuṇa,' he says.
The right [side] belongs to Mitra;
the left belongs to Varuṇa.
The clotted curds belong to the All-Gods;
he brings them down to their own portion.
'Together with all the gods I [stand],' he says.

Offspring belong to the All-Gods;
he makes them eaters.
'Thou art the navel of regal power, thou art the womb of regal power,' saying this, he spreads the upper cover for unity of origin.
'Sit upon the pleasant, sit upon the well-seated [seat],' he says;
this is strictly according to the sacrificial formula.
'May it not harm thee, may it not harm me,' he says, for non-injury.
'Varuṇa, of firm vows, has sat down among his dwellings for universal rule, he of good purpose,' he says;
he makes him one of universal rule, of good purpose.
'O brahman! You, O king, are brahman; you are Savitṛ of true impulse,' he says;
he makes him Savitṛ of true impulse.

'O brahman! You, O king, are brahman; you are Indra of true splendorous might,' he says;
he makes him Indra of true splendorous might.
'O brahman! You, O king, are brahman; you are Mitra the most benevolent,' he says;
he makes him Mitra the most benevolent.
'O brahman! You, O king, are brahman; you are Varuṇa of true ordinances,' he says;
he makes him Varuṇa of true ordinances.
'You are Savitṛ of true impulse,' he says;
with this he proclaims the gāyatrī.
'You are Indra of true splendorous might,' he says;
with this he proclaims the triṣṭubh.

'You are Mitra the most benevolent,' he says;
with this he proclaims the jagatī.
These deities are truth;
these meters are truth;
he secures truth.
'You are Varuṇa of true ordinances,' he says;
with this he proclaims the anuṣṭubh.
The anuṣṭubh is truth and untruth;
Varuṇa is truth and untruth;
he secures truth and untruth.

Truth and untruth, when spoken, do not injure him
who knows thus.
'Thou art Indra's bolt, slayer of Vṛtra,' saying this, he hands him the wooden sword;
for the wooden sword is the thunderbolt;
with the thunderbolt he subdues for him his rivals lower and further.
For so it is better,
that these should be subdued for him.
'This king has overcome the directions,' saying this, he hands him five dice;
these are indeed all the winning throws;
he makes him one who never loses at play.

They proclaim the boiled rice mess [for the king];
he is the highest deity,
namely the boiled rice mess;
he causes him to attain the highest distinction.
'Possessing glorious renown, auspicious, of true kingship!' he says;
this is the blessing he invokes.
He causes the story of Śunaḥśepa to be recited;
he frees him from Varuṇa's noose.
More than a hundred [cows] are given;
man has a lifespan of a hundred [years], a hundred faculties;
he firmly stands in lifespan, in Indra's power.
He cuts portions for Agni Sviṣṭakṛt from [the cake] on twenty-one potsherds for the Maruts and from the clotted curds for the All-Gods;
he encompasses him on both sides with deities.
'To the Son of the Waters, svāhā! To the Son of Vigor, svāhā! To Agni, lord of the house, svāhā!' with this he offers three oblations.
These worlds are three;
he firmly stands in these worlds.

When Varuṇa had consecrated himself [by pressing soma], his Indra's power and heroic power flew away in ten portions.
He crept after it with the creeping [offerings];
that is the creeping-nature of the creeping [offerings].
With Agni as the deity he harnessed [it] on the first day;
with Sarasvatī as speech on the second;
with Savitṛ as impulse on the third;
with Pūṣan as beasts on the fourth;
with Bṛhaspati as brahman on the fifth;
with Indra as the deity on the sixth;
with Varuṇa, his own deity, on the seventh;

with King Soma on the eighth;
with Tvaṣṭṛ as form on the ninth;
with Viṣṇu as the sacrifice he attained it [on the tenth].
In that there are creeping [offerings],
the sacrificer thereby attains Indra's power and heroic power.
Each preceding sacrificial altar becomes higher and higher,
for the securing of Indra's power and heroic power.
Before the upasads he proceeds with [the oblation] to Soma;
for Soma is the placer of semen;
he thus deposits the semen.
Between them [he proceeds] with that to Tvaṣṭṛ;
when semen is placed, Tvaṣṭṛ fashions forms.
Above, [he proceeds] with that to Viṣṇu;
for Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
he firmly stands in the sacrifice at the end.

for the attainment of Prajāpati.
He gives two mirrors to the Adhvaryu;
he causes him to attain renown.
A garland to the Udgātṛ;
he shines forth for him.
A golden breastplate to the Hotṛ;
he leads up the sun for him.
A horse to the Prastotṛ and Pratihartṛ;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati,
for the attainment of Prajāpati.

Twelve four-year-old cows to the Brahman priest;
he secures lifespan.
A barren cow to the Maitrāvaruṇa priest;
he makes the realm submissive to him.
A bull to the Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin priest;
he makes the realm endowed with power.
Two garments to the Neṣṭṛ and Potṛ priests;
these two are his strainers.
A one-ox cart laden with barley to the Acchāvāka priest;
from the very end he appeases Varuṇa.

An ox to the Āgnīdhra priest;
the ox is indeed a bearer,
the Āgnīdhra is a bearer;
by means of the bearer, he secures the bearer of the sacrifice.
When Indra had pressed [soma], his capacity and heroic power flew away in three parts:
Bhṛgu became one-third,
the Śrāyantīya [sāman] one-third,
Sarasvatī one-third.
A descendant of Bhṛgu is the Hotṛ;
the Śrāyantīya is the Brahma-sāman;
the Vāravantīya is the Agniṣṭoma-sāman;
he takes the waters of the Sarasvatī,
for the securing of capacity and heroic power.
The Śrāyantīya is the Brahma-sāman;
he confers capacity and heroic power upon him.
The Vāravantīya is the Agniṣṭoma-sāman;
he secures capacity and heroic power in him.

He is indeed likely to go mad following the quarters,
whom they cause to stand along the quarters.
There are the Aveṣṭis of the quarters;
he becomes established in the quarters, so as not to go mad.
He sacrifices to five deities;
five are the quarters;
he becomes established in the quarters.
After each offering, he causes the Bārhaspatya [portion] to be sprinkled with clarified butter;
Bṛhaspati has the sacrificer as his deity;
he enriches the sacrificer with fiery energy.

He offers a dewlapped, pregnant [heifer] to Aditi,
a speckled four-year-old heifer to the Maruts.
He puts the common people and the realm in harmony for him.
He proceeds with that for Aditi first,
with that for the Maruts afterwards;
he fastens the common people to the realm.
He calls for the Śraukṣat loudly for the one to Aditi,
in a whisper for the one to the Maruts;
therefore the realm commands the common people.
The [heifer] for Aditi is pregnant;

the embryo is capacity;
he makes the realm endowed with capacity.
The [heifer] for the Maruts is not pregnant;
the Maruts are the common people;
he makes the common people devoid of capacity.
The gods and the asuras were in conflict;
the gods, having deposited the truth of speech with the Aśvins and Pūṣan,
overcame the asuras by means of untruth.
They offered a cake on twelve potsherds to the Aśvins and to Pūṣan;
thence they secured the truth of speech.

When he offers a cake on twelve potsherds to the Aśvins and to Pūṣan,
having overcome his rivals by means of untruth,
he secures the truth of speech.
A mess of boiled grain to Sarasvat, whose speech is true;
[what was] spoken before,
he confirms with what follows.
A cake on twelve potsherds to Savitṛ of true impulse, for impulse.
He dispatches messengers;
these are declarations;
they cause him to attain renown;
moreover, he is not cut off from the messengers.
A bow with three arrows and a dry leather bag are the dakṣiṇā, for prosperity.

He offers [a cake] on eight potsherds to Agni;
therefore in the cold season the Kuru-Pañcālas go eastward.
A mess of boiled grain to Soma;
therefore settling down in spring, they feast.
[A cake] on twelve potsherds to Savitṛ;
therefore prior to the barleys they ward [them] off by means of Savitṛ.
A mess of boiled grain to Bṛhaspati;
having warded off by means of Savitṛ,
by means of brahman they set the barleys [to grow].
[A cake] on eight potsherds to Tvaṣṭṛ;

thereby they produce the forms.
[A cake] on twelve potsherds to Vaiśvānara;
therefore in the late summer heat the Kuru-Pañcālas go westward.
He offers a mess of boiled grain to Sarasvatī;
therefore in the rainy season all voices speak.
Through [the offering] to Pūṣan they settle down;
through [the offering] to Mitra they plow;
through [the offering] to Varuṇa they remain restrained;
through [the offering] to Kṣetrapati they ripen [crops];
through [the offering] to Aditi they deposit [grain].

When Indra had pressed [soma], his capacity and heroic power flew away in ten parts.
What he first spat out,
that became the jujube berry;
what [he spat out] the second time,
that became the jujube fruit;
what [he spat out] the third time,
that became the wild jujube berry;
what [came] from his nose,
that was the lion;
what [came] from his two eyes,

that was the tiger;
what [came] from his two ears,
that was the wolf;
what was upward,
that was Soma;
what was downward,
that was the surā liquor.
There are three kinds of groats,
for securing capacity;
[there are] three kinds of hairs.

Prajāpati is unspecified;
[it is] for obtaining Prajāpati.
He draws with a single verse;
he places heroic power in the sacrificer singly.
He seizes a smoke-colored [victim] for the Aśvins;
the Aśvins indeed are the two physicians of the gods;
through the two of them he makes medicine for him.
[He seizes] a ram for Sarasvatī;
Sarasvatī indeed is speech;
by speech indeed he heals him.
[He seizes] a bull for Indra, for possession of Indra's nature.

If he were to seize them at three sacrificial posts,
he would place physical faculty and heroic power outside of him;
he would generate a rival for him.
He seizes them at a single sacrificial post;
he places physical faculty and heroic power in him in one way only;
he does not generate a rival for him.
There are no sacrificial cakes for these domestic animals;
for these are graha-cakes.
With: 'Ye, the surā-drink, O Aśvins', the two offering verses belong to all the gods;
he pleases all the deities.

He should hire a Brahmin as the drinker of the remnant;
for the Brahmin is the drinker of the remnant of the oblation.
If he should not find a Brahmin,
he should pour it down into an anthill;
that indeed is the atonement for it.
Whatever is defective in the Sautrāmaṇī,
that becomes successful for him.
There are domestic animals and sacrificial cakes dedicated to various deities for success.
The one for Indra is the last of the domestic animals;
the one for Indra is the first of the sacrificial cakes.

He places in him physical faculties that correspond to one another.
He proceeds with the sacrificial cakes before the after-offerings;
the sacrificial cakes indeed are domestic animals;
he secures domestic animals.
He pours out [the grain for] an eleven-potsherd [cake] for Indra;
he secures physical faculty.
[He pours out grain for] a twelve-potsherd [cake] for Savitṛ for impulse;
[and grain for] a ten-potsherd [cake] for Varuṇa;
at the end indeed he appeases Varuṇa by sacrifice.
A mare is the sacrificial fee.

For she brings forth both a horse
and a mule;
both soma and surā;
that the Sautrāmaṇī is for success.
For one excessively purified by soma, he seizes a domestic animal for Bṛhaspati as a fourth;
Bṛhaspati indeed is the brahman of the gods;
by the brahman he remedies what is defective in the sacrifice.
This domestic animal is provided with a sacrificial cake;
for they do not draw a cup of this [animal's liquor].
Saying: 'O fathers with Soma as front, be satiated', he pours it down together into a [vessel] perforated with a hundred holes.

A person has a lifespan of a hundred [years], a hundred faculties;
he establishes himself in lifespan and physical faculty.
He offers in the southern fire,
for the separation of good and evil.
He holds gold in between;
he offers it having been purified.
It is of a hundred measures;
a person has a lifespan of a hundred [years], a hundred faculties;
he establishes himself in lifespan and physical faculty.
Where he holds the [vessel] perforated with a hundred holes,

He undertakes the Agniṣṭoma at first;
the Agniṣṭoma indeed is the opening of the sacrifice;
having begun at the opening of the sacrifice, he advances to the consecratory rite.
Then this consecratory rite has a thirty-four-fold Pavamāna;
thirty-three indeed are the deities;
he attains them;
Prajāpati is the thirty-fourth;
him indeed he attains.
This is a destruction of the chants out of normal order,
in that the chants are uneven.

The sacrifice is just as much
as the Pavamānas are.
Another, however, is the interior fastening;
in that the Pavamānas are even;
thereby there is no destruction;
thereby it is according to the normal order.
Through himself, through the Agniṣṭoma, he thrives;
through himself he becomes pure.
The Ukthas indeed are offspring;
the Ukthas are domestic animals;
in that it is an Ukthya, [it is] for continuous succession.

The introductory verse is: 'Unto thee the sister voices.'
Vāyu indeed is speech;
this royal unction is of speech itself.
He is consecrated for all offspring;
all offspring address him as 'King'.
He says: 'And that one, the ten fingers.'
Offspring indeed are of Āditya;
by this he is consecrated for offspring.
They go away from the opening of the sacrifice,
those who made the materials to be brought together.

In that he says: 'Flow, chief of speech',
thereby they do not go away from the opening of the sacrifice.
An anuṣṭubh is the first;
an anuṣṭubh is the last;
speech indeed is the anuṣṭubh;
by speech indeed they go forward;
by speech they rise up.
They are [verses] containing the word 'up';
containing the word 'up' indeed is the form of the anuṣṭubh;
the Rājanya is connected with the anuṣṭubh.

in the morning to both;
he does not set conflict among the deities.
He says: 'Agni is light.'
Agni, indeed, is the placer of seed.
'Offspring is light,' he says;
he propagates offspring for him.
'Sūrya is light,' he says;
he places seed in the offspring that have been born.
'Light is Agni, svāhā,' he says;
he establishes the born offspring firmly upon this [earth].

that belongs to domestic animals;
in that he offers,
that possesses spiritual preeminence;
he accomplishes both.
Fallen away from this world,
not yet arrived at the world of the gods,
is the cooked oblation that is unanointed with clarified butter.
He illumines it;
he pours clarified butter over it;
thus he causes it to go to the gods.

He carries fire around it,
for the warding off of the Rakṣases.
Three times he carries fire around,
for the sacrifice is threefold,
and also for fitness for sacrifice.
If he were to remove it toward the east,
he would inflict the sacrificer with grief;
if toward the south,
it would belong to the realm of the Fathers;
if toward the west,

he would inflict the wife with grief.
He removes it toward the north;
this indeed is the peaceful direction of gods and men;
toward it he removes it for peace.
He makes a path,
for the continuity of the sacrifice.
He scorches it over the fire;
thereby he strews beneath it.
Four times he ladles it out;
four-footed are domestic animals;

one hands it to another,
such indeed is that;
he would be cut off from him.
In that he sets it over the Gārhapatya fire,
thereby he gratifies the Gārhapatya.
Agni was afraid:
'The oblations will pass beyond me.'
He saw this kindling stick;
he placed it upon [the fire];
thereupon indeed the oblations stayed in Agni.

From the Ṣaḍḍhotṛ he fashioned the Saptahotṛ formula.
He employed it.
Upon its employment, he became a soma-drinker, a soma-sacrificer;
a soma-drinker, a soma-sacrificer was born to him.
Whoever should desire: 'May I be a soma-drinker, a soma-sacrificer;
may a soma-drinker, a soma-sacrificer be born to me,'
he should employ the Saptahotṛ formula.
He indeed becomes a soma-drinker, a soma-sacrificer;
a soma-drinker, a soma-sacrificer is born to him.
This domestic animal indeed stands firmly in five ways:

Discussers of sacred lore say:
'What is the caturhotṛ-nature of the Caturhotṛ formulas?'
Because the hotṛ priests are fourfold in them,
therefore they are the Caturhotṛs;
therefore they are called Caturhotṛs.
That is the caturhotṛ-nature of the Caturhotṛ formulas.
Soma indeed is the Caturhotṛ;
Agni is the Pañcahotṛ;
Dhātṛ is the Ṣaḍḍhotṛ;
Indra is the Saptahotṛ;

He exhausts his vital breaths.
If they should invite him back,
he should offer the Daśahotṛ formula in the Āgnīdhra fire itself
with ghee scooped four times,
sitting in the west facing east,
forwards and without dividing [the mantras];
he properly arranges the vital breaths for him.
One performing an expiation offers 'Vāc is the hotṛ' at the beginning of each season;
he properly arranges the seasons for him;
the seasons are properly arranged for him;

he conquers all quarters.
Prajāpati is the hotṛ of the ten-hotṛ formula;
Soma is the hotṛ of the four-hotṛ formula;
Agni is the hotṛ of the five-hotṛ formula;
Dhātṛ is the hotṛ of the six-hotṛ formula;
Aryaman is the hotṛ of the seven-hotṛ formula.
These indeed are the hotṛs of the cāturhotṛ formulae.
He who knows them thus
eats food among all creatures;
he conquers all quarters.

these of his self he rescues,
and goes to unison with Āditya.
Four times he scoops up;
twice he offers:
he obtains that rite of sacrifice which is the six-hotṛ,
the animal sacrifice;
the two breasts, the two testicles, the penis, and the downward breath—
these of his self he rescues,
and goes to unison with Āditya.
Four times he scoops up;
twice he offers;

The conveyor of oblations, who overpowers malice, / the slayer of demons, victorious in battles, / luminous, shining, wise, / Agni, the maker of proper offerings [we invoke].
Accomplish that properly offered [rite], O Agni; / overcome all hostiles, O god; / shine out, showing us the broad path; / grant us luminous, unaging [life]!
To you, O Agni, the mortals bearing oblations / sing praise, to you the god;

From the ocean, from the far distance; / may the one blow skill to me, / may the other blow away whatever is ailment.
Whatever treasure of immortality / is placed there in your house, O wind, / therefrom give us to live; / therefrom grant us healing; / therefrom bring us greatness!
May the wind blow healing to us, / bringing welfare, bringing bliss to our heart;

May the sons [of heaven] listen to our songs,
they who [are born] of truth;
may they be very gracious to us.
Today, divine Savitṛ,
send us good fortune with offspring;
drive away bad dreams.
Divine Savitṛ, drive away
all evils;
send us whatever is auspicious.
Bṛhaspati, pure, with songs of praise,

pay homage to at the sacrificial rites;
I celebrate his unyielding splendorous might.
Which the two gods of good skill, dakṣa's fathers, upheld
for asuric power, of eminent might.
He indeed dwells safely in his own home;
to him the sacrificial refreshment swells at all times;
to him the clans bow down of their own accord,
in whose kingdom the Brahmin walks in front.
With design, O Indra, with shaking,
with shaking, with buttock-shaking,

the desired, dear [portion] of the sacrifice.
"I give," says Agni;
"So be it," says Vāyu to that;
"Yes!" says the moon in truth;
the sun [says], "Indeed, truth, om!"
The waters brought that truth,
the dignity, the sacrificial gift of the sacrifice;
may this desire of mine be fulfilled.
For they found no other spy than him,
than Agni Vaiśvānara, as the forerunner;

the intermediate realm has spread wide;
the high heaven yields milk.
They are not lost, nor does the thief harm [them];
no hostile assailant assails them with distress;
and the master of cows with whom he worships the gods and gives [them],
with them he associates for a long time.
The dust-raising courser does not reach them;
they do not go toward slaughter;
along his broad-striding, fearless path,
the cows of that mortal sacrificer wander.

Night, approaching, has looked out
in many places with her eyes, the goddess;
she has assumed all glories.
Unto you, as [unto] cows, I have brought
a hymn of praise, O daughter of heaven;
Night, accept it like a victor.
The gods generated the divine Speech;
her the domestic animals of every form speak;
may she, the delightful cow Speech yielding to us refreshment and vigor,
well-praised, come unto us.

When Speech, speaking things incomprehensible,
the ruler of the gods, delightful, sat down,
she yielded as milk four invigorations, the waters;
whither indeed has her highest [form] gone?
The buffalo cow lowed, fashioning the floods,
becoming one-footed, two-footed, four-footed,
eight-footed, nine-footed,
thousand-syllabled in the highest firmament.
From her the oceans flow forth;
by that the four directions live;

from that flows the imperishable [syllable];
upon that the universe subsists.
Begetting the heroic Indras, Viśvakarman,
may he be with the Maruts, with a troop, with kin;
may the daughter-in-law, the co-wives, with mind
attend to the command, the speech of this father-in-law.
Indra, the hero, crossed the dust realms;
may this [man] be the father-in-law, the daughter-in-law, the co-wife;
may he conquer the enemies, exulting;
may he conquer prize in the winning of prize.
Agni, bearer of regal power, of unbent splendorous might,
possessed of thousands, may he blaze without neglect;
shining brightly, kindled, fierce,
he mounted the intermediate realm, he went to heaven.

You are a bull shoot; you are drawn for the bull;
a bull is this fierce one, for the watcher of men;
heavenly, suitable for the rite, laid down, great indeed is the name
which is the hump of the bull.
May he become central, O Viṣṇu,
this one who is my bull;
then, like Indra to the gods,
may he proclaim to the people,
possessing a long lifespan, possessing lustre,
and the overlord of the clans.

He whose oblation this bull is,
led around for Indra,
shall conquer the approaching enemy,
and slays those who fight against him.
Truly he shall be the leader of men;
he shall be at the head of those who burst forth.
O Indra, stir up vigor in your body;
trample down all assailants;
crush down whatever distress is ours;
make wide space for us, O liberal giver.

Go forth, advance, bring forward, overpower;
do not turn away, make yourself heard among the people;
arise, praised, O bull, with your vigors;
O Indra, fight the enemies before us.
O Agni, conqueror, conquer you
the enemies with power, with splendorous might;
drive away the enemies, the foes.
This song of praise for you, O of mighty birth, I, an inspired poet,
have fashioned like a skilled craftsman a chariot, O wise one;
if indeed, O god Agni, you should welcome it,

Rise up, you who possess prizes, who are within the waters;
enter this realm full of pleasant truth.
Rohita, who begot this universe,
may he establish us securely in realms.
Ascent after ascent Rohita mounted,
growth through offspring, the lap of births;
supported by them, he found the six wide regions;
foreseeing the path, you brought the realm here.
Rohita has brought the realm here;
he has dispersed the foes; may there be safety for us.

You, fierce Maruts, whose mother is Pṛśni,
crush the enemies together with Indra as your ally;
Rohita heard you, O heavenly ones,
thrice seven Maruts, rejoicing in sweetness.
Rohita begot heaven and earth;
in them Parameṣṭhin stretched out the warp;
in them Ajā Ekapād took his support;
he strengthened heaven and earth with power.
Rohita strengthened heaven and earth;
by him the sun is propped, by him the firmament.

He is the measurer of the space in the intermediate region;
through him the gods found the sun.
The flames shining upon you who are very gracious,
united as ladles, O Jātavedas,
sprinkle you, the prize-winner, with ghee;
you bring together nourishments, O Agni.
O Agni, be confident for great good fortune;
may your splendors be supreme;
make the household easy to guide;
trample upon the powers of those who act as enemies.

the conqueror of enemies, observing all.
For intention, for desire, for fulfillment,
I place you in front, for immortality, for living.
For his intention, for assistance,
for the fulfillment of his desire,
they yoke the thoughts of Indra.
The divine Intention I place before the mind;
may the mother of the sacrifice be easy to invoke for me;
what I desire in my mind with longing,
may I find this residing in my heart.

Bring to us, Indra, radiant good fortune;
may we abide in the surplus of your gift;
like an abyss, desire has expanded in us;
fill that, O lord of possessions, with goods.
Delight this desire with cows and horses,
and expand it with brilliant reward;
seeking heaven through their thoughts for you, the sages,
the Kuśikas have made a vehicle for Indra.
Now I will proclaim the heroic deeds of Indra,
which the vajra-wielder performed as the first ones.

He slew the serpent; he drilled through following the waters;
he split open the bellies of the mountains.
He slew the serpent lying upon the mountain;
Tvaṣṭṛ fashioned for him the heavenly vajra;
like lowing cows flowing,
the waters went straight down to the sea.
Acting like a bull, he chose the soma;
he drank of the pressed [soma] in the Trikadruka [rites];
the generous one took the vajra missile;
he slew him, the first-born of the serpents.

When, O Indra, you slew the first-born of the serpents,
then you confounded the illusions of the illusionists,
and then, bringing forth the sun, the sky, the dawn,
truly then you found no enemies at all.
Indra slew Vṛtra, the greater vṛtra, with shoulders crushed,
with his vajra, the great weapon;
like tree trunks hewn apart with an axe,
the serpent lay clinging to the earth.
For, intoxicated, he challenged [Indra] as if unable to fight,
to the great hero, the conqueror, the drinker of soma dregs.

O goddess of the forest, O goddess of the forest yonder, / you who seem to vanish away: / how is it that you do not ask for the village? / Does not fear, as it were, overcome you?
When the ciccika bird answers / the crying cricket, / as if rushing with cymbals, / the goddess of the forest is glorified.
And it seems as if cattle are eating, / and it seems as if a dwelling is seen;

Auspicious to you be both these, heaven and earth;
auspicious to you be Agni together with the waters; / auspicious to you be the four quarters.
The divine four quarters / whose lord is the wind, upon which the sun looks: / to them I commit you until old age; / let consumption depart far off to Nirṛti! / He has been released from consumption, from evil, from distress;

For a long lifespan, for one of a hundred autumns,
I accept [it], for great heroic power.
You are lifespan, you are all lifespan,
you are complete lifespan, you are entire lifespan.
May my entire lifespan be fulfilled;
may I attain my entire lifespan.
Bhūḥ! Bhuvaḥ! Suvaḥ!
Agni by righteousness is the eater of food;
Death by righteousness is the lord of food;
brahman, regal power, svāhā!

Prajāpati is the guide;
Bṛhaspati is the vanguard;
Yama is the path;
the Moon is the reviver of breath, svāhā!
Agni is the eater of food, the lord of food;
may he give food to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Soma is king, the lord of kings;
may he give kingship to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Varuṇa is the sovereign, the lord of sovereigns;
may he give sovereignty to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!

Mitra is regal power, the lord of regal power;
may he give regal power to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Indra is strength, the lord of strength;
may he give strength to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Bṛhaspati is brahman, the lord of brahman;
may he give brahman to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Savitṛ is dominion, the lord of dominion;
may he give dominion to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Pūṣan is the lord of the clans of the clans; may he give the clan to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Sarasvatī is nourishment, the mistress of nourishment;
may she give nourishment to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!
Tvaṣṭṛ, the maker of forms for pairs of domestic animals, is the lord of forms;
may he give domestic animals in [proper] form to the sacrificer at this sacrifice, svāhā!

Drink this, you who are residueless, victorious,
who are full-cheeked, a bull among the inspired,
who are breaker of pens, wielder of the thunderbolt, who stand on bay horses;
O Indra, open up wondrous spoils of victory.
May your bull-like fury come from behind,
from the north, from below, from the front;
may it assemble toward us from all sides;
O Indra, bestow on us heavenly splendor.
Advance toward him, in front of the chariot;
sing of fury to Indra!

O Maruts, [sing] to the foremost slayer of Vṛtra,
with whom the increasers of truth produced light,
the watchful god for the god.
What are these drizzles, what are these winged insects?
Locusts swarm around me in a flock;
may the gods blow them away unimpeded;
may I obtain the eye of an eagle in my body.
Thus praise the lofty Varuṇa,
reverence the wise guardian of immortality;
may he extend to us threefold shelter.

grant to me from that;
may I partake of that of yours;
may we become the greatest sharers in that of yours.
I am from you, you are from me in this;
you are my womb, I am your womb;
being mine, O Agni, convey the oblations,
as a son creating wide space for his father, O Jātavedas.
Being right here, while you are there, O Agni,
I sustain you with exhalation, speech, and mind;
while I am hidden, may lifespan not abandon me.

I attend you with the light of Vaiśvānara.
This is your seasonable womb,
from which born you shone;
knowing it, O Agni, mount it,
and then increase our wealth.
With that body of yours that is worthy of sacrifice, O Agni, come, mount your self,
preparing many manly deeds for us;
having become the sacrifice, sit upon the sacrifice, your own womb;
O Jātavedas, being born of the earth, come with your dwelling!
Step down again, O Jātavedas!

The divine Indra, the tree of the forest,
golden-leaved for the two Aśvins,
with fine berries for Sarasvatī;
for Indra, the sweet drink is cooked.
Like splendorous might, speed; like a bull, fury;
may the tree of the forest establish indriyas for us.
For the winning of wealth, let them partake of the gift of wealth; sacrifice!
The divine sacred grass of the waters,
strewn at the sacrificial rite for the two Aśvins,
soft as wool for Sarasvatī;

A pleasant seat for you, O Indra;
they established wrath for the sovereign, the king, indriya through the sacred grass.
For the winning of wealth, let them partake of the gift of wealth; sacrifice!
May the divine Agni Sviṣṭakṛt
sacrifice to the gods according to their order;
the two Hotṛs, the two Aśvins for Indra,
with speech, speech to Sarasvatī,
Agni, Soma, the Sviṣṭakṛt;
well-sacrificed is Indra Sutrāman, Savitṛ, Varuṇa the physician;
sacrificed to is the divine tree of the forest;
well-sacrificed are the gods who drink clarified butter;
sacrificed to is Agni by Agni;
the Hotṛ, the Sviṣṭakṛt for the priest,
establishing indriya like renown,
vigor, reverence, the svadhā-call.
For the winning of wealth, let them partake of the gift of wealth; sacrifice!

The Fathers, releasing from distress, devoted to soma,
becoming immortal, both higher and lower,
may they speak in our favor, may they protect us,
enjoying the porridge mixed with the milk of the newly calved cow;
Arising, the lower and the higher,
consumed by fire, in agreement with the seasons,
accompanied by Indra, may they enjoy this oblation.
When, O Agni, bearer of ancestral oblations; you, O Agni, praised, O Jātavedas;
Mātali with the ancestral offerings;
who, yearning among the gods, took delight;
thriving on the priest's call, fashioned through praise-songs with sacred chants;
Come hither, O Agni, with the benevolent,
true Fathers, eaters of the kavya, seated at the hot cauldron;
Serving Agni, the conveyor of oblations, ageless, dear to many,
with clarified butter, with the offering,
I have approached the ancestral oblation-bearer of the Fathers;
May he unite us with offspring rich in heroes!

All songs have magnified Indra, / wide as the sea, / the greatest charioteer among charioteers, / the true lord, the master of prizes.
May the sounds of the army / and the greedy bowstrings bypass me; / standing at the post, swelling here, / let them enter into me, the lord of cattle.
May Anumati agree to that for me; / may mother earth and father heaven agree to that;

May Soma give a milch cow, may Soma [give] a swift steed;
may Soma give a heroic son, skillful in action,
fit for the home, fit for the assembly, fit for the council,
bringing fame to his father, who serves him.
[The pratīkas:] "Unconquered in battles," "You, O Soma, by your insights,"
"Those forms of yours they sacrifice to with the oblation."
You, O Soma, [created] all these plants;
you brought forth the waters, you the cattle;
you extended the broad intermediate space;
you uncovered the darkness with light.

Everything firm trembles, shaken by him;
the waters flowed in accordance with his own nature.
He grew in the midst of navigable waters;
with a concentrated mind, O Indra, with the mightiest
blow you struck him through the days.
Accompanied by the Maruts, the bull growing great,
not withholding, the heavenly commander, Indra,
all-conquering, for new aid,
that mighty giver of strength let us summon here;
he was born mighty for swift power.

That which is your lunar mansion, Mṛgaśīrṣa,
dear, O King, dearest of the dear,
to that [mansion] of yours, O Soma, may we pay worship with oblation;
be peace to our two-footed, peace to our four-footed!
Rudra comes expanding with Ārdrā,
the best of the gods, the lord of the unslayable [cows];
may we pay worship to his lunar mansion with oblation;
may he not harm our offspring nor our heroes!
May the missile of Rudra pass around us;
may the Ārdrā lunar mansion enjoy our oblation!

Bṛhaspati, being born first,
came into being over the Tiṣya lunar mansion,
the best of the gods, victorious in battles;
along all quarters may there be fearlessness for us!
May Tiṣya protect us in front and in the middle;
may Bṛhaspati protect us from behind!
May the two ward off enmity, may they make fearlessness;
may we be masters of heroic strength!
May this oblation be pleasing to the serpents,
whose intent the Āśreṣās follow!

Aryaman, the unaging, mighty king,
the bull of the Phalgunīs, roars loudly.
Best of the gods, O Bhaga, you are Bhaga;
the Phalgunīs know that of you; know that!
Push together here for us unaging regal power,
accompanied by heroic strength, rich in cows, rich in horses!
Bhaga indeed is the giver, Bhaga is the distributor;
Bhaga entered the divine Phalgunīs;
may we go to that instigation of Bhaga,
where we may revel in joint revelry with the gods!

He places light in the intermediate space;
he places light as the sun in yonder world.
Luminous become these worlds for him
who knows thus.
"You are steadfast; make firm the earth," he says;
by this he makes firm the earth.
"You are a support; make firm the intermediate space," he says;
by this he makes firm the intermediate space.
"You are a foundation; make firm the sky," he says;
by this he makes firm the sky.

"You are order; make firm the directions," he says;
by this he makes firm the directions.
By these he makes firm these worlds.
These worlds become firm for him with offspring and domestic animals,
who knows thus.
At first he sets down three potsherds;
three are these worlds,
for the attainment of these worlds.
At first he sets down one potsherd;
one potsherd [of the skull], indeed, first comes into being in a man.

that [is commensurate] with the Jagatī.
Setting down the potsherds commensurate with the meters,
he makes firm these worlds in order, and the directions for support.
Then he places lifespan, breaths, offspring, and domestic animals in the sacrificer;
he makes his kinsmen abundant all around him.
"You are piled up," he says;
this is just as the formula [prescribes].
"Be heated by the heat of the Bhṛgus and the Aṅgirases," he says;
he heats them with the heat of the deities.
Then, when it is concluded,
with the four-footed verse, "The potsherds which the wise arrange in the heat," he unlooses [them].
Domestic animals are four-footed;
he firmly establishes [himself] upon domestic animals in the end.

"At the impulse of the divine Savitṛ," he says, for impelling.
"With the arms of the Aśvins," he says;
for the Aśvins were the Adhvaryus of the gods.
"With the hands of Pūṣan," he says, for guiding.
"I pour you together," he says;
according to the deities he pours them together.
"Waters have met with waters, plants with sap," he says.
The waters quicken the plants;
the plants quicken the waters;
they quicken each other.

"You are the caldron, having all life," he says;
he places all life in the sacrificer.
"Spread wide; may the lord of the sacrifice spread wide," he says;
he spreads wide the sacrificer with offspring and domestic animals.
"Take on a skin," he says;
he makes it completely endowed with a body.
Then, having brought water, he smooths it around;
he thereby places skin upon the flesh.
Therefore the flesh is covered by skin.
This caldron, indeed, unappeased,

is placed upon the fire half-month by half-month,
[namely] that which is the sacrificial cake.
It is capable of burning up the sacrificer with heat.
He carries fire around [it];
he makes it an animal,
for appeasement, so that it will not burn up.
Three times he carries fire around [it];
for threefold is the sacrifice,
and also for the driving away of the demons.
"Warded off is the demon, warded off are the enemies," he says,

He covers it with the broom;
therefore the head is covered with hair.
He does not become bald
who knows thus.
The sacrificial cake is the likeness of the sacrificial animal.
It must not be covered without a formula;
it would be [done] at random;
the domestic animals of the sacrificer would be liable to perish.
"Unite with brahman," he says.
The vital breaths, indeed, are brahman.

‘Deposit your bodies in me;
I will generate for you him
on whom you shall wipe [it] off.’
Those gods deposited their bodies in Agni.
Therefore they say:
‘Agni is all the deities.’
With an ember he cast water [into the coals];
thence Ekata was born.
He cast a second [drop];

thence Dvita was born.
He cast a third [drop];
thence Trita was born.
Because they were born from the waters (āpaḥ),
that is the nature of the Āpyas (āpyatva).
Because they were born from the bodies (ātman),
that is the nature of the Ātmyas (ātmyatva).
Those gods wiped off [the pollution] on the Āpyas;
the Āpyas wiped [it] off on one upon whom the sun rises [while sleeping];
he upon whom the sun rises, on one upon whom the sun sets [while sleeping];

he upon whom the sun sets, on a bad-nailed man;
the bad-nailed man, on a dark-toothed man;
the dark-toothed man, on an agradidhiṣu;
the agradidhiṣu, on a parivitta;
the parivitta, on a killer of a hero;
the killer of a hero, on a killer of a Brahmin; that [pollution] did not pass beyond the killer of a Brahmin.
He pours [the wash water] within the sacrificial altar to secure [it].
He holds [the cake] over with a firebrand for cookedness;
for the gods indeed desire cooked food.

‘At the impulse of god Savitṛ,’ he says, [and] takes the wooden sword for instigation.
‘With the arms of the Aśvins,’ he says;
for the Aśvins were the two Adhvaryus of the gods.
‘With the hands of Pūṣan,’ he says for restraint.
‘I take [thee]; thou art Indra’s right arm,’ he says;
he places virile power into the sacrificer.
‘Thousand-spiked, hundred-pointed,’ he says;
he describes this very form [and] greatness of it.
‘Thou art Vāyu, sharp-pointed,’ he says;
Vāyu is fiery energy;

he places fiery energy into this [sacrificer].
There was an asura named Viṣādh.
He was afraid:
‘The gods will overcome me with sacrifice.’
He vomited upon the earth;
she became unhallowed.
Moreover, when Indra slew Vṛtra,
his blood ran across the earth;
she became unhallowed.
‘O earth, place of divine worship,’ he says;

he makes her hallowed, a place of divine worship.
‘May I not harm the root of thine plant,’ he says,
for the non-injury of the plants.
‘Go to the fold, the cow-pen,’ he says;
the meters are the fold, the cow-pen;
he makes the meters a fold, a cow-pen for him.
‘May the sky rain upon thee,’ he says;
the sky is rain;
he secures rain.
‘Bind [him], O god Savitṛ, in the furthest distance,’ he says;

he strikes him away from the intermediate space.
He carries away a third [handful];
he strikes him away from heaven.
He carries away a fourth [handful] silently;
he strikes him away from the unmeasured.
In the beginning this [earth] belonged to the asuras;
as far as a seated man looks out,
so much [alone] belonged to the gods.
Those gods said:
‘Let there be [a share] in this for us as well.’

‘How much will you give us?’ [they asked].
‘As much as you can enclose yourselves,’ [replied the asuras].
They enclosed it from the south, [saying,] ‘May the Vasus [enclose] thee!’;
from behind, [saying,] ‘May the Rudras [enclose] thee!’;
from the north, [saying,] ‘May the Ādityas [enclose] thee!’
With Agni they conquered eastward;
with the Vasus southward;
with the Rudras westward;
with the Ādityas northward.
For him who knows thus, when they enclose the sacrificial altar,

he prospers in himself;
his rival is defeated.
‘At the impulse of the god Savitṛ,’ he says for instigation.
‘The wise perform the work,’ he says;
for a work is performed when impelled.
The hallowed and the unhallowed went apart on the earth:
the eastward and northward is hallowed,
the westward and southward is unhallowed.
He makes [the altar] slanting toward the east and north;
he makes her hallowed, a place of divine worship.

He raises the two shoulders of the sacrificial altar toward the east
for the enclosure of the Āhavanīya fire;
[he makes] the two hips toward the west
for the enclosure of the Gārhapatya fire;
and also for pairing.
He digs [the earth];
whatever of it is unhallowed,
that he strikes away.
He digs; therefore plants perish.

He cuts the root;
he cuts the very root of the rival.
If the root were left standing, the rakṣases would leap up along with it.
If he were to cut it with his hand,
his offspring would be bad-nailed.
He cuts with the wooden sword;
the wooden sword is indeed a vajra;
with the vajra he strikes away the rakṣases from the sacrifice.
If dug too deep, it belongs to the ancestors as deities;
he digs only so much:

measured with Prajāpati, the mouth of the sacrifice.
The sacrificial altar hid from the gods;
they found it at [a depth of] four finger-breadths.
Therefore it should be dug four finger-breadths [deep].
He digs four finger-breadths [deep];
for plants are established at four finger-breadths.
He digs for a firm foundation;
he brings the sacrificer to a firm foundation.
He makes it higher on the south side;
he has made the very form of the place of divine worship.

If he should set them near from east to west,
he would cause the kindling wood to arrive away from the path of the oblations.
The sacrificial grass is offspring;
he would miss the procreation of offspring with the sacrificial grass.
He sets them near from west to east;
he causes the kindling wood to arrive by the path of the oblations;
he meets the procreation of offspring directly with the sacrificial grass.
The kindling wood is to the south,
the sacrificial grass to the north.
The kindling wood is the self;
the sacrificial grass is offspring;
for offspring are farther north of oneself at the crossing place.
Having brought the sacrificial substance near from there,
he establishes it according to each deity.
The sacrificer firmly establishes himself with offspring and domestic animals.

'Burned away is the rakṣas, burned away are the spiteful foes,' he says,
to strike away the rakṣases.
'With the hottest fiery energy of Agni I scorch you,' he says for fitness for sacrifice.
He cleanses the offering ladles:
the dipping spoon first;
he prepares a male for them for the sake of pairing;
then the juhū;
then the upabhṛt;
then the dhruvā.
Yonder [sky] is the juhū;

the intermediate space is the upabhṛt;
the earth is the dhruvā.
These worlds are the offering ladles;
the cleaning grasses are rain.
Rain prepares these worlds in order;
then, prepared, they prosper.
These worlds prosper with offspring and domestic animals for him
who knows thus.
If he should desire that Parjanya should become rainy,
he should cleanse from the tip.

For this [arm] is an offering ladle.
The dipping spoon is exhalation;
the juhū is the right hand;
the upabhṛt is the left;
the dhruvā is the self;
the cleansing grasses are food.
Having become inhalation at the mouth,
food, having entered the self,
beautifies the body from without.
Therefore he cleanses the dipping spoon first;
for having become inhalation at the mouth,
food enters the self.
They are exhalation and inhalation.
He is not deprived of exhalation and inhalation
who knows thus.

'The artwork of the sky stretched down,
resting on the crest of the earth,
with that thousand-branched one
we destroy the rival, svāhā,' [with this formula] he throws the cleaning grasses of the ladles into the fire.
Darba grasses are water;
he thus proclaims their form and their greatness.
With an anuṣṭubh verse [he throws them];
Prajāpati is connected with the anuṣṭubh;
the broom of grass belongs to Prajāpati;
the cleaning grasses of the ladles are the top of the broom.

He equips them with their own meter,
with their own deity.
Moreover, the verse is female;
darbha grass is male;
that is a pair.
He thus makes a pair of it in the sacrifice for procreation.
The sacrificer propagates with offspring and domestic animals.
Some cast them away at random;
that must not be done so.
That would be a draining of the sacrificial act that has been undertaken.

the chaff-particles are redundant;
the leftover clarified butter is redundant.
In the redundant, he places the redundant;
moreover, having obtained the redundant by the redundant, he secures it.
The sacrificial bed hid from the gods.
They found it by means of the grass broom.
By the grass broom they found the sacrificial bed, the earth;
she, the earth, spread out earthly things;
she bears the embryo within the creatures;
therefrom the sacrifice is born, all-bestowing.
Before the grass-bush formula, he sweeps the sacrificial bed with the grass broom for finding [it].

Moreover, as there are the grass broom and the sacrificial bed,
[it is] for pair-formation, for procreation.
These are the whiskers of Prajāpati,
namely, the grass broom.
He throws it into the lap of the [sacrificer's] wife;
he makes a pair;
he obtains offspring.
The Hotṛ goes strewing the grass broom from the āhavanīya fire;
thereby he connects the sacrifice up to the next half-month;
he takes hold of it continuous in the next half-month.

He prays for this blessing at the full vessel.
At the end [he does so] with an anuṣṭubh;
this four-footed meter is established for the wife at the full vessel,
thinking: 'May I be established in this world.'
She becomes established in this very world.
Moreover, speech is the anuṣṭubh;
speech is a pair;
the waters are seed, procreative.
From this pair, flashing and thundering, [the cloud] rains,
pouring seed,

For brahman, he seizes a Brahmin;
for regal power, a Rājanya;
for the Maruts, a Vaiśya;
for ascetic fervor, a Śūdra;
for darkness, a thief;
for hell, a slayer of heroes;
for evil, a eunuch;
for buying, an Ayogū;
for desire, a courtesan;
for extreme outcry, a bard from Magadha.

For song, a Sūta;
for dance, an actor;
for dharma, an assembly-goer;
for jest, a chatterer;
for danger, a violent man;
for laughter, a clown;
for bliss, a companion of women;
for delight, the son of an unmarried maiden;
for intelligence, a chariot-maker;
for steadfastness, a carpenter.

For toil, a potter;
for magical craft, a smith;
for beauty, a jewel-maker;
for splendor, an unguent-maker;
for the arrow-shot, an arrow-maker;
for the weapon, a bow-maker;
for action, a bowstring-maker;
for fate, a rope-maker;
for death, a hunter;
for the ender, a dog-keeper.

For union, a paramour;
for the home, a second husband;
for ruin, a younger brother married before the elder;
for distress, an elder brother unmarried after the younger;
for failure, the husband of an elder sister whose younger sister has married first;
for purification, a physician;
for wisdom, an astrologer;
for adornment, an embroideress;
for strength, an oblation-bearer;
for color, a tracker.

For rivers, a Pauñjiṣṭha;
for bears, a Niṣāda;
for the man-tiger, a reckless brawler;
for madnesses, a madman;
for Gandharvas and Apsarases, a Vrātya;
for serpent-deities, one who cannot pace in meter;
for the Avī deities, a gambler;
for wanderlust, a non-gambler;
for Piśācas, a basket-weaver;
for Yātudhānas, a maker of thorns.

For destructions, a hunchback;
for delight, a dwarf;
for door-posts, a lame man;
for sleep, a blind man;
for unrighteousness, a deaf man;
for concord, an inciter of love;
for arbitrary speech, an attendant;
for study, an interrogator;
for apprentice study, a cross-examiner;
for boundaries, an arbitrator of questions.

For strife, a thief at heart;
for manslaughter, an informer;
for discrimination, a distributor;
for oversight, a charioteer;
for strength, a follower;
for abundance, an attendant;
for the dear, a speaker of pleasant words;
for security, a horse-rider;
for sacrifice, a washerwoman of clothes;
for desire, a woman dyer.

For light, a wood-gatherer;
for radiance, a fire-kindler;
for the back of the firmament, a consecrator;
for the summit of the sun, a vessel-cleanser;
for the world of the gods, a carver;
for the world of men, a scatterer;
for all the worlds, a sprinkler;
for distress and slaughter, a churner;
for the heavenly realm, a distributor of shares;
for the highest firmament, a server of meals.

For ruins, an elephant-keeper;
for speed, a horse-keeper;
for nourishment, a cowherd;
for fiery energy, a goat-herd;
for heroic power, a shepherd;
for food, a cultivator;
for sweet drink, a liquor-maker;
for good fortune, a house-guard;
for welfare, a guardian of wealth;
for superintendence, an assistant distributor.

For wrath, a metal-smelter;
for anger, a pursuer;
for grief, an attacker;
for steep slope and descent, a three-legged cripple;
for exertion, a yoker;
for rest, an unyoker;
for physical form, an artist;
for virtue, an ointment-maker;
for ruin, an embroideress;
for Yama, a barren woman.

For Yamī, a mother of twins;
for the Atharvans, a woman who has miscarried;
for the year, a wandering woman;
for the Parivatsara, a woman who has not given birth;
for the Idāvatsara, a woman who leaps over boundaries;
for the Idvatsara, a woman who transgresses;
for the Vatsara, a decrepit woman;
for the Sarvaṃtsara, a gray-haired woman;
for the forest, a forest-guard;
for the wilderness beyond, a wildfire-warden.

For lakes, a fisherman;
for ponds, a boatman;
for stagnant pools, a Bainda;
for reed-beds, a dried-fish dealer;
for the near bank, a Kaivarta;
for the far bank, a Mārgāra;
for fords, an Ānda;
for uneven places, a Maināla;
for sounds, a Parṇaka;
for caves, a Kirāta;
for mountain ridges, a Jambhaka;
for mountains, a Kiṃpuruṣa.

For the echo, an imitator;
for noise, a barking dog-keeper;
for the end, a babbler;
for the endless, a mute;
for greatness, a lute-player;
for clamor, a flute-blower;
for wailing, a drum-beater;
for all around, a conch-blower;
for the Ṛbhus, a skin-stitcher;
for the Sādhyas, a leather-worker.

For loathsomeness, a Paulkasa;
for prosperity, a wakeful man;
for misfortune, a sleeper;
for the balance, a merchant;
for color, a goldsmith;
for the All-gods, a leprous man;
for subsequent guilt, a dullard;
for strife, a slanderer;
for ruinous failure, an insolent man;
for destruction, a wood-cutter.

For laughter, he seizes a courtesan;
a lute-player and an astrologer for song;
for sea-monsters, a speckled woman;
for jest, a gracious woman;
a flute-blower, a headman, and a hand-clapper for dance;
for joy, a comforter;
for bliss, a clapper.

For the King of Dice, a gambler;
for the Kṛta age, a frequenter of assemblies;
for the Tretā age, an observer of vices;
for the Dvāpara age, an outsider;
for the Kali age, a permanent fixture of the assembly;
for evil deed, a roving teacher;
for the road, a Vedic student;
for Piśācas, a stone-cleaver;
for thirst, a cow-tormentor;
for ruin, a cow-slayer;
for hunger, a cow-carver;
for hunger and thirst, that one
who approaches begging for meat while one carves up an ox.

For the earth, he seizes a cripple who moves on a stool;
for Agni, a brawny man;
for Vāyu, a Cāṇḍāla;
for the intermediate space, a bamboo-dancer;
for heaven, a bald man;
for the sun, a yellow-eyed man;
for the moon, a blinking man;
for the lunar mansions, a spotted man;
for the day, a fair man with reddish-brown eyes;
for the night, a dark man with reddish-brown eyes.

For speech, he seizes a man;
exhalation, inhalation, diffused breath, upward breath, and concentrated breath—these for Vāyu;
for the sun, he seizes the eye;
the mind for the moon;
for the directions, the ear;
for Prajāpati, a man.

Forward [go] your prizes, shining every day,
accompanied by oblations, with the [ladle] flowing with ghee;
seeking favor, he goes to the gods.
O Agni, come to the feast,
being praised, to the giving of oblation;
sit down as Hotṛ upon the strew.
As such, O Aṅgiras, with firesticks
and with ghee we magnify you;
glow greatly, O youngest one!
May he [bring] us broad, renowned [wealth].

Agni is the head, the summit of heaven,
this lord of the earth;
he quickens the seeds of the waters.
You became the leader of the sacrifice and of the atmospheric realm,
where you attend with your propitious span of steeds.
You placed your head in heaven, winning the sun;
you made your tongue the bearer of oblations, O Agni.
O Prajāpati, none other than you
has encompassed all these beings born;
with whatever desire we sacrifice to you, let that be ours.

May we be lords of riches!
He knows the father, he the mother, as the son;
he shall become the son, he shall become repeatedly giving gifts;
he covered the sky, the intermediate space, and the light of heaven;
he became all existences, he came into being.
O Agni and Soma, of one accord,
accept our songs with one invocation;
you came into unison among the gods.
You placed these lights in heaven,
Agni and Soma, of one resolve.

He should press it down with the bottom of the sruva spoon:
'Do not faint; let not the sacrifice faint; let not the sacrificer faint.
Homage be to you coming;
homage, O Rudra, to you departing;
homage to where you sit down;
do not harm so-and-so, do not harm so-and-so'—referring to the one towards whom it jumped.
He should cast it [into the fire]:
'The thousand-horned bull, Jātavedas,
backed by chants, rich in ghee, fair-faced,
may he not forsake us, when addressed let him not forsake us;
bestow on us increase of cattle and increase of heroes.'
With the brahman he casts it.
That is the expiation for it.

He says: "Agni";
Agni, indeed, is the placer of seed;
he places seed indeed.
He says: "Indra";
he places manly power into him.
He says: "Tvaṣṭṛ";
Tvaṣṭṛ, indeed, is the fashioner of forms for pairs of domestic animals;
he places form in the domestic animals.
He says: "Bṛhaspati";
Bṛhaspati, indeed, is the brahman of the gods;
by the brahman he generates offspring for him.
He says: "It has dripped down onto the earth";
he establishes it in this [earth]. He says: "It does not reach Nirṛti turning away";
[it is] for the warding off of demons.

May Agni, carrier of oblations, bring them here;
this full-moon oblation of theirs is in me;
this new-moon oblation of theirs is in me.
Between the two fires, the domestic animals
have come to the assembly of the gods;
those I grasp first
in [their] own abode with thought.
May all-formed offspring rest here,
dwelling around Agni, the master of the house;
those I grasp first

in [their] own abode with thought.
May all-formed domestic animals rest here,
dwelling around Agni, the master of the house;
those I grasp first
in [their] own abode with thought.
This fire of the ancestors
has carried the oblations to the ancestors;
that [fire] I grasp first;
may it make our food free from poison.
May the guardians of the hall continuously

who have Indra as their chief.
Here I announce the sacrifice to the Ādityas,
who have Varuṇa as their chief.
Rich in milk are the plants;
rich in milk is the sap of the herbs;
that sap which is the milk of the waters—
with that, O Indra, unite me.
O Agni, lord of vows, I will perform the vow;
may I be able to do that; may that succeed for me.
O Vāyu, lord of vows! O Āditya, lord of vows!

subordinate to the dhruvā spoon is this intention of his.
May I be superior;
may my rivals be beneath me.
You are the mighty bull,
son of the butter-flowing [ladles].
Sit in your dear seat with your dear name;
sit kindly for me, easy to sit upon on the earth.
Extend [me] with offspring, with domestic animals, in the heavenly realm;
sit in heaven, on earth, in the intermediate space.
May I be superior;

may my rivals be beneath me.
This pot is full of ghee,
a fountain of a hundred streams with unceasing milk.
With divine shelter through the Maruts,
you are the sacrifice, supported on every side;
may what has been and what will be support me on every side.
May a hundred blessings be mine;
may a thousand pleasant words be mine,
full of refreshments, full of domestic animals.
You are Prajāpati, supported on every side;

may what has been and what will be support me on every side.
May a hundred blessings be mine;
may a thousand pleasant words be mine,
full of refreshments, full of domestic animals.
This is the immortal, potent heroic power;
by this, the domestic animals were discerned for Indra.
Thereby, O gods, favor me.
Here may I win refreshment, vigor, glory, victorious power, and splendorous might.
May what is cooked rest in me;
that which moved over the earth has entered it.

Wherewith Prajāpati poured strength into Indra,
this is that bright, prize-winning sweetness;
wherewith he pleased Mahendra from above,
may the curds please me.
This sacred broom found the earth,
which was hidden in the deep, in recesses;
may it find a world for the sacrificer;
may it of manifold actions make the sacrifice free from defect.
This sacrifice, possessing oblations, has taken its place,
with the Ṛg verse, the Sāman, the Yajus, and with the deities.

for winning the prize I wipe down;
Agni, the eater of food, for the eating of food.
Invoked is Father Sky;
may Father Sky invoke me;
Agni from the Agnīdhra fire,
for lifespan, spiritual preeminence, life-sustenance, and holiness.
Invoked is Mother Earth;
may Mother Earth invoke me;
Agni from the Agnīdhra fire,

for lifespan, spiritual preeminence,
for life-sustenance, and holiness.
May mind, the light, relish the clarified butter;
may it put together this broken sacrifice;
may Bṛhaspati extend this [sacrifice] of ours;
may the All-gods rejoice here.
Whomever, O Agni, I cut off,
or if I, being injured, wander about,
both his offspring and his root
do you, O gods, cut off downward.

A peg of heaven driven down,
arisen from the earth,
with that of a thousand joints
we cause the hater to grieve.
May my hater grieve much;
O herb, may I not grieve.
O sacrifice, homage to thee, O sacrifice;
homage and homage to thee, O sacrifice.
Conclude for me propitiously;
conclude for me kindly;

conclude for me with prosperity;
conclude for me with spiritual preeminence;
conclude following the success of the sacrifice.
Near to thee, O sacrifice, is homage;
near to thee is homage;
near to thee is homage.
Of [the grains] being husked three times,
whatever remainder is left behind,
the portion of the rakṣases—
may the waters carry that away from here.

Whatever has stuck in the mortar, in the pestle, and on the winnowing basket,
what on the millstone, what on the potsherd,
the falling drops, the scattered particles, I offer together;
may the All-gods relish this oblation.
Whatever many drops there are in the sacrifice,
all those I offer in the fire, well-sacrificed, well-poured.
Rising today, O Mitra of vast might,
destroy my rivals;
strike them with lightning from heaven;
setting, make them low.

Rising today, share out to us
as a father to his sons;
for thou rulest over long life,
grant us that, O Sūrya.
Rising today, O Mitra of vast might,
mounting the higher heaven,
my heart disease, O Sūrya,
and the jaundice make vanish.
My jaundice to the parrots
and to the starlings we transfer;

He sacrifices with the Sāṃgrahaṇī offering,
[thinking:] ‘May I comprehend this community.’
The halter is twelve cubits long;
the year has twelve months;
he secures the year itself.
It is made of muñja-grass;
muñja-grass is vigor;
he secures vigor itself.
Citrā is the lunar mansion;
wonderful indeed is this rite:

namely, the horse sacrifice, for success.
He settles upon a place of divine worship with an auspicious name;
thereby he wins auspicious renown.
They bring along the priests, except those on foot, up to the Subrahmaṇyā chant,
for the attainment of the heavenly realm.
He shaves his hair and beard;
he trims his nails;
he cleans his teeth;
he bathes;
he puts on an unwashed garment,
for the removal of evil.
Restraining his speech, he fasts,
for the guarding of the heavenly realm.
They sit keeping watch through the night,
for the attainment of the heavenly realm.

There are four kinds of water;
four-hoofed is the horse belonging to Prajāpati, for success.
They are collected from the quarters;
water is in the quarters;
water is food;
food is born from water;
whatever food is born from water,
that he secures.
In them he cooks the boiled rice mess for the priests;
thereby he deposits semen.

It consists of four dishes;
he is firmly established in the quarters.
There are gold plates on both sides;
he bestows brilliance upon him on both sides.
He lifts it off, for it to be well-cooked;
it has clarified butter, for sacrificial fitness.
Four men of ṛṣi descent eat of it;
he offers into the light of the quarters.
He gives four pieces of gold;
he secures the lights of the quarters.

"Having sacrificed with this sacrificial horse,
may this king attain his full lifespan!"
Lifespan indeed is the Udgātṛ;
lifespan are the chamberlains and charioteers.
With lifespan he thus places lifespan in him.
They are a hundred each.
A man has a lifespan of a hundred [years] and a hundred powers;
he thus firmly establishes [him] in lifespan and in power. There are four hundred [in all];
four are the quarters;
he firmly establishes [him] in the quarters.

Just as indeed [part] of an oblation that has been scooped out spills,
so indeed of this horse [a part] spills,
when, having been washed, they let [him] go unseized.
When he recites the drops-verses,
he renders him entirely offered, for the sake of non-spilling;
for that is unspilled,
whatever spills of what is offered.
He recites a thousand [verses];
commensurate with a thousand is the heavenly realm,
for the winning of the heavenly realm.

If he should recite a measured number,
he would win what is measured.
He recites an unmeasured number;
unmeasured is the heavenly realm,
for the attainment of the heavenly realm.
He offers the drops-oblations;
whatever waters are of rain,
those he secures.
He offers on this [earth];
this [earth] indeed is the fire Vaiśvānara.

He thus establishes them firmly upon this [earth].
Prajāpati said:
"In the drops-oblations indeed I conclude the Horse Sacrifice;
having concluded it through that, I thence go about."
He says: "To Agni, svāhā!";
he offers him to Agni.
He says: "To Soma, svāhā!";
he offers him to Soma.
He says: "To Savitṛ, svāhā!";
he offers him to Savitṛ.

He says: "To Sarasvatī, svāhā!";
he offers him to Sarasvatī.
He says: "To Pūṣan, svāhā!";
he offers him to Pūṣan.
He says: "To Bṛhaspati, svāhā!";
he offers him to Bṛhaspati.
He says: "To the delight of the waters, svāhā!";
he offers him to the waters.
He says: "To Vāyu, svāhā!";
he offers him to Vāyu.

He says: "To Mitra, svāhā!";
he offers him to Mitra.
He says: "To Varuṇa, svāhā!";
he offers him to Varuṇa.
He offers him to these deities.
He offers them to the completion of tens.
The Virāj has ten syllables;
food is the Virāj;
through the Virāj he secures food.
He falls away from this world
who offers oblations away [from himself];
he offers turning back again and again;
he firmly establishes himself in this world.
He indeed proclaimed this completion of the Horse Sacrifice for the sake of non-spilling;
for that is unspilled,
whatever spills of a sacrifice that has been completed.

"Sprinkled acceptable to Prajāpati [are you]," standing on the east facing west, he sprinkles [him];
Prajāpati is the eater of food, full of heroic power among the gods;
he thus places food and heroic power in him;
therefore the horse is the greatest eater of food and has the greatest heroic power among domestic animals.
"To Indra and Agni [are you]," [he sprinkles him] from the south;
Indra and Agni are the most splendidly mighty and the strongest among the gods;
he thus places splendorous might and power in him;
therefore the horse is the most splendidly mighty and the strongest among domestic animals.
"To Vāyu [are you]," [he sprinkles him] from the west;
Vāyu is the swiftest, the most fleet-running among the gods;

he thus places speed in him;
therefore the horse
is the swiftest, the most fleet-running among domestic animals.
"To the Viśve Devas [are you]," [he sprinkles him] from the north;
the Viśve Devas are the most renowned among the gods;
he thus places renown in him;
therefore the horse is the most renowned among domestic animals.
"To the gods [are you]," [he sprinkles him] from below;
the gods are the most honored among the gods;
he thus places honor in him;
therefore the horse is the most honored among domestic animals.

"To all the gods [are you]," [he sprinkles him] from above;
all the gods are brilliant and furious;
he thus places brilliance and fury in him;
therefore the horse is brilliant and the most furious among domestic animals.
"To heaven [are you], to the intermediate space [are you], to earth [are you]," he says;
he sprinkles him for these worlds.
"To the existent [are you], to the non-existent [are you], to the waters [are you], to the plants [are you], to all beings [are you]," he says;
therefore all beings live upon one who sacrifices with the Horse Sacrifice.
The discussers of sacred lore say:
"Since the horse belongs to Prajāpati,
why then does he sprinkle him for other deities as well?"
In the horse indeed all deities are contained.
When he sprinkles him [saying], "To all beings [are you],"
he makes the deities inhere in him;
therefore all deities are contained in the horse.

Just as indeed [part] of an oblation that has been scooped out spills,
so indeed of this horse [a part] spills,
when, having been sprinkled, they let [him] go unseized.
When he offers the horse's activities,
he renders him entirely offered, for the sake of non-spilling;
for that is unspilled,
whatever spills of what is offered.
He says: "To the sound 'īm', svāhā! To that which is accompanied by the sound 'īm', svāhā!";
these indeed are the horse's activities;
with these activities he makes him prosper.

On this point they say:
"The horse's activities are non-oblations;
these should not be offered."
And then, on the contrary, they say:
"They must indeed be offered;
here indeed, knowing thus, one concludes the Horse Sacrifice,
when he offers the horse's activities;
therefore they must be offered."
[Otherwise] he places him outside of his proper abode;
he generates a rival for him.

‘If he were to offer at each mouth of the sacrifice,
he would deprive the sacrificer of domestic animals;
he would fall down from the heavenly realm;
he would become worse.’
They should be offered only once.
He does not deprive the sacrificer of domestic animals;
he wins the heavenly realm;
he does not become worse.
He offers forty-eight horse-forms.
The jagatī metre consists of forty-eight syllables;
the horse belongs to the jagatī, [is] sacred to Prajāpati, for prosperity.
He offers one extra;
therefore, one among offspring is thriving.

‘Extending by mother, prevailing by father,’ he says.
This [earth] indeed is the mother;
yonder [sky] is the father.
To these two he delivers him.
‘You are a horse, you are a courser,’ he says;
he thereby instructs him;
therefore well-instructed offspring are born.
‘You are a racer,’ he says;
therefore the horse surpasses all domestic animals;
therefore the horse attains preeminence among all domestic animals.

He attains fame [and] preeminence,
who knows thus.
‘You are a male, you are a steed, you are a runner, you are a courser,’ he says;
he thereby proclaims his form [and] his greatness.
‘Yayur is your name,’ he says;
that indeed is the horse’s dear name;
with his dear name he addresses him.
Therefore, even two enemies having met,
if they call [each other] by name,
become friends indeed.

‘Follow the flight of the Ādityas,’ he says;
he causes him to go to the Ādityas.
‘To Agni svāhā! Svāhā to Indrāgni!’ thus he offers the preliminary offerings;
he walks ahead past the hating rival.
‘You are being, to being thee, to what has been thee, to what will be thee!’ thus he releases [him], for wholeness.
‘O gods that guard the quarters, protect this horse consecrated for sacrifice for the gods,’ he says.
A hundred sons of kings on couches, indeed, are the gods that guard the quarters;
to them he delivers him.
Capable indeed is the released horse of going to the furthest distance.
‘Here is support, svāhā! Here is restraint, svāhā! Here is delight, svāhā! Here is revelry, svāhā!’—thus he offers at the four feet.

These indeed are the horse’s bond;
with them he binds him;
therefore the horse, though released, comes to the tether;
therefore the horse, though released, does not abandon the tether.
The horse sacrifice is indeed the realm;
they strive hard in the realm,
who guard the horse for the sacrifice.
Those among them who reach the conclusion,
they go from realm to realm;
but those who do not reach the conclusion,
they are cut off from the realm.
He is indeed poured away
who, being weak, sacrifices with the horse sacrifice.
If enemies should find the horse,
his sacrifice would be destroyed.
Four hundred [men] protect [it],
for the non-destruction of the sacrifice.
Then, bringing another, they should sprinkle [it];
that indeed is the expiation thereafter.

Prajāpati desired: ‘May I sacrifice with the horse sacrifice.’
He performed ascetic fervor.
From him, as he was heating himself,
seven deities went upward out of his self;
that became the initiation.
He saw these Vaiśvadeva [offerings];
he offered them;
by them he obtained the initiation.
When he offers the Vaiśvadeva [oblations],
by them the sacrificer obtains the initiation.

He offers seven,
for seven were those deities that departed.
He offers day by day;
day by day he secures the initiation.
He offers three Vaiśvadeva [oblations],
four elevation offerings;
they amount to seven.
Seven indeed are the vital breaths in the head;
the vital breaths are the initiation;
by means of the vital breaths he secures the vital breaths, the initiation.

He offers twenty-one Vaiśvadeva [oblations];
twenty-one indeed are the divine realms:
twelve months, five seasons,
these three worlds;
yonder sun is the twenty-first.
This is the heavenly realm;
that is divine regal power;
that is royal majesty;
that is the summit of the sun;
that is called self-sovereignty.

He offers thirty elevation offerings;
the virāj consists of thirty syllables;
the virāj is food;
by means of the virāj he secures proper food.
Dividing it threefold, he offers to the deity.
Threefold indeed are the gods;
threefold are these worlds;
for the attainment of these worlds,
for the proper arrangement of these worlds.
The vital breaths depart from him,

who lets the initiation run over.
They perform [the rite] for seven days;
seven indeed are the vital breaths in the head;
the vital breaths are the initiation;
by means of the vital breaths he secures the vital breaths, the initiation.
He offers a full oblation at the end.
The full oblation is indeed the all;
he attains the all.
Moreover, this [earth] indeed is the full oblation;
he firmly establishes himself on it.

They pen up mares,
for pairing, for procreation;
moreover, that is just as assistant singers accompany the singing.
'The sacrificial horse has chanted,' he says.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati;
Prajāpati is the udgītha;
he secures the udgītha;
moreover, he firmly establishes himself in the ṛc and the sāman.
He presents [it] with gold.
Gold is light;
he places light in the front,
both in the sacrificer and in his offspring;
moreover, with the light of gold the sacrificer goes to the heavenly realm.

Therefore they establish the front fire in the east.
One dedicated to Pūṣan behind [it];
Pūṣan is food;
therefore they bring the food-portion to the front fire.
One dedicated to Indra and Pūṣan above;
the Rājanya is connected with Indra, Pūṣan is food;
with food he grasps him from both sides;
therefore the Rājanya tends to be an eater of food.
Two dedicated to Agni, black-necked, on the two forelegs;
he places heroic power in the two arms;

therefore the Rājanya tends to be strong in the arms.
Two dedicated to Tvaṣṭṛ, with hairy thighs, on the two hind thighs;
he places heroic power in the two thighs;
therefore the Rājanya tends to be strong in the thighs.
Two white-backed ones dedicated to Bṛhaspati on the back;
he places spiritual preeminence on top;
moreover, these two encompass [him] on both sides like two coats of mail;
therefore the Rājanya, clad in armor, performs heroic power.
For Dhātṛ, one with a speckled belly underneath;
he makes this a firm foundation;
moreover, this [earth] is Dhātṛ;
he establishes himself on this [earth].
A white one dedicated to Sūrya at the tail;
he makes that a stronghold;
therefore subjects resort to a stronghold in times of danger.

the sacrificer, having reached the Virāj, obtains her with them.
Eleven decades [of animals] are seized.
The Triṣṭubh has eleven syllables;
domestic animals belong to the Triṣṭubh;
he thus obtains domestic animals.
The horse is sacred to all gods.
The domestic animals have various deities,
for the completeness of the horse.
They are of varied forms;
therefore domestic animals are of varied forms.
They are multi-formed;
therefore domestic animals are multi-formed, for prosperity.

are there many desires in this world?’
Because different animals are seized for the same deities,
he thereby places desires in this world;
therefore in this world there are many desires.
He offers the omentum of three [animals] together.
Threefold are the gods;
threefold are these worlds,
for the attainment of these worlds,
for the proper ordering of these worlds.
They release the wild ones after carrying fire around them, for non-injury.

‘They harness the tawny one,’ he says.
That sun, indeed, is the tawny one;
he harnesses the sun for him.
‘The ruddy one,’ he says.
Agni, indeed, is the ruddy one;
he harnesses Agni for him.
‘The moving one,’ he says.
Vāyu, indeed, is the moving one;
he harnesses Vāyu for him.
‘Standing around,’ he says;

these worlds, indeed, stand around;
he harnesses these worlds for him.
‘The lights shine in heaven,’ he says.
The lunar mansions, indeed, are the lights in heaven;
he causes the lunar mansions to shine for him.
‘They harness his beloved ones,’ he says.
He harnesses his desires for him.
‘The two bay steeds on either side,’ he says.
These two [worlds], indeed, are the two bay steeds on either side;
he harnesses these two for him.

‘Crimson, bold, bearing men,’ he says.
Day and night, indeed, are the two that bear men;
he harnesses day and night for him.
These deities he harnesses for him,
for the attainment of the heavenly realm.
‘Making a banner for that which has no banner,’ he fastens on the flag;
he makes him attain glory among kings.
‘It becomes like the face of a cloud,’ he says;
this is in accordance with the yajus.
‘Those paths of yours, O Savitṛ, which are ancient,’ the Adhvaryu makes the sacrificer recite, for conquering.

With ‘bhūr, bhuvaḥ, svar,’ sacred to Prajāpati, they weave [them] in.
The horse is sacred to Prajāpati;
with its own deity they cause it to thrive.
‘Bhūr’—the chief queen;
‘bhuvaḥ’—the favorite wife;
‘svar’—the discarded wife;
for the conquest of these worlds.
The beads are made of gold.
Gold is light;
the horse sacrifice is sovereignty.

and they fan him;
and they make amends to him.
Three times they go around: three are these worlds;
they fan him from these worlds.
Three times again they go around;
they amount to six;
six are the seasons;
they fan him by means of the seasons.
The vital breaths depart from them

who perform the fanning at the sacrifice.
Nine times they go around.
Nine are the vital breaths in man.
They place the vital breaths in themselves;
the vital breaths do not depart from them.
With "O mother, little mother, dear mother!" he leads forward the wife.
He calls her.
"O fortunate one, dweller in Kāmpīla," he says;
he brings her to austerity.
"Cover yourselves together in the heavenly realm," he says;

this is just as the formula.
There are three kinds of needles:
of iron, of silver, and of gold.
The iron ones are the form of this world;
the silver ones, of the atmosphere;
the gold ones, of the sky.
The iron ones are the cardinal directions;
the silver ones, the intermediate directions;
the gold ones, the zenith.
He arranges the directions for him.
"Who cuts you? Who carves you?" he says, for non-injury.

You, Agni, are Rudra, the asura of high heaven;
you rule as the troop of the Maruts, the rich feast;
you travel along with the ruddy winds, bringing blessing to the home;
you as Pūṣan protect the worshipers now yourself.
Gods, rest upon the gods!
First ones, rest upon the second ones!
Second ones, rest upon the third ones!
Third ones, rest upon the fourth ones!
Fourth ones, run to the fifth ones!
Fifth ones, rest upon the sixth ones!

sacrifice to the gods for him who is devoted to the gods.
For Agni gives a courser to the clan,
he who turns towards all peoples,
Agni, who is well-abiding for wealth;
he, being pleased, goes to what is choice;
bring refreshment to the praisers.
Questioned in heaven, questioned on earth,
Agni, questioned, entered all herbs;
Agni Vaiśvānara, questioned with might—
may he protect us by day, may he [protect us] from injury by night.

He conquers re-death,
who piles the Nāciketa fire,
and who knows it thus.
Prajāpati, desiring offspring, performed ascetic heat.
He cast forth gold.
He cast it into the fire;
it did not please him.
He cast it a second time;
it did not please him at all.
He cast it a third time;

it did not please him at all.
He cast it into the Vaiśvānara fire within his own heart;
it pleased him.
Therefore gold is the smallest of riches,
[yet] the dearest when enjoyed,
for it was born from the heart.
Indeed, he found no one
to whom he could lead that sacrificial gift;
he led it to his own right hand;
he received it.

"To you, O most Angiras-like..."; "May we attain that desire, O Agni...";
"To you of the quarters..."; "All the quarters...";
"May Anumati approve of us today..."; "Do you, O Anumati, approve...";
"Desire of what has come to be..."; "Desire in the beginning...";
"The brahman born first..."; "Father of the Virājs...";
"The sacrifice of wealth..."; "This sacrifice...";
"Auspicious waters..."; "Then indeed I see...";
"To you they bring, who the mud-built [ramparts]...";
"The gods formerly by what is later..."; "Exhalation and inhalation...";
"The bearer of oblations..."; "Well-sacrificed..."

These indeed are the seven doors of the heavenly realm,
named the Falcons of Heaven, the Findings.
Hope guards the first;
Desire the second;
brahman the third;
Sacrifice the fourth;
Waters the fifth;
Agni Possessed-of-Tribute the sixth;
Finding the seventh.
He indeed finds the heavenly realm;
his movement is at will in the heavenly realm,
who sacrifices with these offerings,
and who knows them thus.
Upon these offerings,
he should give a four-year-old cow as choice portion, and a brass vessel,
and an ornament for a woman, for prosperity.

Through ascetic heat the gods attained divinity in the beginning;
through ascetic heat the seers found the sun.
Through ascetic heat let us drive away rivals and foes,
by which this all, whatever exists, is encompassed.
Let us worship the first-born god with an oblation,
the self-existent brahman, which is the highest ascetic heat.
He indeed is the son, he the father, he the mother;
as ascetic heat indeed did the first wonder come into being.
Through faith a god attains divinity;
faith is the foundation, the divine queen of the world.

so that we may revel together with the gods.
Whose foundation is the broad midspace,
from whom the gods and all beings were born,
that Truth, shining, has come to our sacrifice,
brahman rejoicing at the offerings.
Under the control of Mind this all has come to be;
Mind never goes under the control of another.
For formidable is the god, mightier than might;
may he, being pleased, come to our sacrifice.
The overlord of intentions and thoughts,

The heavenly realm became hidden from the gods.
They said to Prajāpati:
'Prajāpati, the heavenly realm has become hidden from us;
seek it.'
He sought it with the sacrificial rites;
he did not find it with the sacrificial rites.
He sought it with the offerings;
he found it with the offerings.
That is the offering-nature of the offerings.
They are indeed called searchings;
them they call offerings cryptically,
for the gods are fond of the cryptic.

Agni is the Hotṛ of the Pañcahotṛ formulas;
Speech is the Hotṛ of the Ṣaḍḍhotṛ formulas;
Mahāhavis is the Hotṛ of the Saptahotṛ formulas.
This indeed is the Caturhotṛ nature of the Caturhotṛ formulas,
and also the Pañcahotṛ nature.
To him indeed all quarters yield,
who knows thus.
This indeed is the all-knowledge;
this is the medicine;
this is the fivefold straight path leading to the heavenly realm.

With them he conquered disputation,
and all evil;
he went to heaven.
Whoever studies these
conquers disputation,
and all evil;
he goes to heaven.
With them, one desiring the heavenly realm should pile the fire altar;
with them, one desiring a complete lifespan,
or one desiring offspring and domestic animals.

In front he lays down the Daśahotṛ [bricks] facing north, according to the words,
and the heart, the two Yajuses, and the two wives;
to the south, the Caturhotṛ [bricks] facing east;
to the west, the Pañcahotṛ [bricks] facing north;
to the north, the Ṣaḍḍhotṛ [bricks] facing east;
above, the Saptahotṛ [bricks] facing east,
and the heart, the Yajuses, and the wives;
according to their spaces the Grahas,
according to their spaces the Pratigrahas and the Lokaṃpṛṇā bricks.
All these deities become pleased and propitiated for him;

he piles the fire altar accompanied by the gods.
It should be piled to the measure of a chariot;
the chariot is indeed a vajra;
by means of the vajra he lays low his evil rival.
It should be piled to the measure of the side wing [of a chariot];
as large as the side wing,
so large indeed is the chariot;
he piles it just to the measure of a chariot.
This world indeed he conquers by an animal sacrifice,
and by the Agniṣṭoma;

the midspace by the Ukthya;
heaven by the Atirātra;
all worlds by the Ahīna rite,
and by the sattra.
A boon is the dakṣiṇā;
by a boon he redeems a boon,
for oneself is a boon.
He gives twenty-one as the dakṣiṇā;
twenty-onefold indeed from here is the heavenly realm;
he attains the heavenly realm.

That sun is the twenty-first;
he attains that sun.
He gives a hundred;
man has a lifespan of a hundred years and a hundred faculties;
he is established in lifespan and faculties.
He gives a thousand;
the heavenly realm is commensurate with a thousand;
[it is] for the winning of the heavenly realm.
He gives the dakṣiṇā according to the bricks,
[representing] all ages of life,

for the attainment of everything,
for the securing of everything.
If he should not find [them],
he should give that many stirred drinks or boiled rice messes;
he attains that wish
for the sake of which wish the fire altar is piled.
Rather, he should give a pregnant four-year-old heifer,
for she is indeed all ages of life,
for the attainment of everything,
for the securing of everything.

He gives gold;
possessing the light of gold indeed, he goes to the heavenly realm.
He gives a garment;
thereby he lengthens his lifespan.
He should sacrifice on the third of the altar;
for the gods are triply true;
he piles the fire possessed of truth.
One should recite this Brāhmaṇa at the animal sacrifice,
not at other sacrifices.
Whosoever indeed knows the four Hotṛs to be satisfied at every pressing,

He is satisfied with offspring and domestic animals;
the drinking of soma bends down toward him.
These, truly, are the Caturhotṛs to be satisfied at every pressing:
those Brahmins who know much.
If he should not bring a sacrificial gift to them,
it would be an imperfect sacrifice;
they would strip away his fire.
He should give to them according to his faith;
thereby this [sacrifice] is made well offered;
they do not strip away his fire.

The eastern is called the great quarter of the verses;
the southern they declare to be the boundless [quarter] of the sacrificial formulas;
the western belongs to the Atharvans and Angirases;
the northern is called the great quarter of the chants.
By verses the god travels in heaven in the forenoon;
he stands in the Yajurveda
at the middle of the day;
by the Sāmaveda he is magnified at sunset:
not devoid of the three Vedas, the sun moves.
All form, they say, is born of the verses;
all motion is indeed ever belonging to the sacrificial formula;

all fiery energy has indeed ever the nature of the chants;
all this indeed was created by brahman alone.
The Vaiśya class, they say, was born of the verses;
the Yajurveda they call the womb of the Kṣatriya;
the Sāmaveda is the origin of the Brahmins:
this word the ancients spoke to the ancients.
Piling the conspicuous fire,
the ancient all-creators, immortal,
consecrated for a hundred thousand years,
sat at the sacrificial session.

Austerity was the householder;
brahman itself became the Brahman priest;
truth indeed was their Hotṛ priest,
when the all-creators sat [at the sacrifice].
Immortality chanted the Udgītha for them
for a thousand full-years;
the past indeed was their Prastotṛ priest;
and the future responded as the Pratihartṛ.
Exhalation became the Adhvaryu priest,
as they sought to win all this:

inhalation, knowing the ritual course,
stood as the Pratiprasthātṛ at the sacrificial rite.
The seasonal [deities] were the Upagātṛ choristers;
the seasons became the Sadasyas;
and the half-months and the months
became the Camasādhvaryus.
The fiery energy of brahman recited as Śastṛ;
renown became the Acchāvāka priest;
truth-order was their Praśāstṛ priest,
when the all-creators sat [at the sacrifice].

Vigor brought up the king [Soma];
overcoming power was the Dhruvagopa;
splendorous might praised the pressing stones,
when the all-creators sat [at the sacrifice].
Reverence sacrificed the Potṛ's share;
brilliance sacrificed the Neṣṭṛ's share;
knowing truth from the Āgnīdhra hearth,
Faith herself indeed offered the sacrifice.
Food-refreshment was the wife of the all-creators;
intention pounded the oblation:

hunger and thirst, both formidable,
brought fuel to them.
Speech was their Subrahmaṇyā priestess,
knowing the meters' combinations,
weaving the ritual frameworks and day-closures on all sides
and the closures.
Day and night were the animal guardians;
the moments became the attendant-messengers;
Death then was the placer [of the victim];
the fierce lord of the clans was the slaughterer.
